{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '363,640p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md\"","aggregated_output":"## Extension (2026-10-19, design only): the engine execution host for guest machines\n\nStatus: design, owner-authorized planning; not implemented. The existing\ndocument body (§1–§8 above) remains authoritative for landed `yin.vm.engine`\nbehavior.\n\nConsensus provenance (2026-10-18): gpt-6-astra, author; gemini-4-argon,\nreviewer; five review rounds, APPROVE on r5. Author thread:\n`01a14f30-ca56-7530-bb93-3a870eca7a7b`; reviewer thread:\n`f365ef1d-caa2-4379-8e96-51e64ca36af8`. It does not report an\nimplementation or runtime acceptance result.\n\nOwner amendment, decomposition, and fold notes (2026-10-19):\n1. **Owner ruling (2026-10-19, ABI naming and host home)**: The\n   host-independent execution content lives in `yin.vm.engine` (\"since its\n   `yin.vm.engine` then it should be `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`\"), and the\n   service ABI is renamed `yin.register.host.v1` → `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`.\n2. **Owner ruling (2026-10-19, document fold)**: By explicit owner ruling,\n   `docs/design/yin.vm.register-wasm.md` and\n   `docs/design/yin.vm.conformance-kit.md` are retired and folded into the\n   existing architecture documents:\n   - This extension (`§E.1–§E.5`) holds the guest-agnostic execution-host\n     contract, the canonical `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` service protocol tables,\n     and the **conformance method** (`§E.5`, folded from\n     `yin.vm.conformance-kit.md` `§1–§5`, `§8`).\n   - [`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) `§10`\n     holds the register machine's specific wasm execution instance (folded from\n     `yin.vm.register-wasm.md` `§1–§5`) together with the register fixture\n     schema and register W0–W6 / Rust K1–K5 lane gates (folded from\n     `yin.vm.conformance-kit.md` `§2`, `§6`, `§7`).\n   - [`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md) holds register-image-to-WebAssembly\n     lowering and the 23 opcode lowerings. Normative content is otherwise\n     frozen.\n\nAuthorities and companion specifications:\n- [datom.world.md](./datom.world.md): foundational axioms and invariants.\n- [yin.vm.semantic.md](./yin.vm.semantic.md): A identity and semantic execution.\n- [yin.vm.debruijn.register.md](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md): register contract, live sets, sparse continuations, and (§10) the register wasm execution instance, register fixture schema, and register W0–W6 / Rust K1–K5 conformance gates.\n- [yin.vm.linker.md](./yin.vm.linker.md): admission, offset tables and module transport.\n- [yin.vm.rust-kernel.md](./yin.vm.rust-kernel.md): shared Rust crates, Value ownership, transition reuse and LLVM relationship.\n- [yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md): lowering register images to WebAssembly modules, targeting `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`.\n\n### E.1 The machine-state-as-data boundary and synchronization protocol\n\nA guest machine's execution state must materialize as plain host-readable\ndata at every service boundary; the host never reaches into guest-private\nstorage. Each task owns one imported, non-shared wasm32 linear-memory arena\nas a private execution cache; separate tasks receive separate arenas even\nwhen compiled Module objects are cached. A supplied `coremem` can implement\nthe same interface only with task isolation and allocation ownership; shared\nmutable guest state is not introduced.\n\nThe public CLJS VM remains a persistent record with ordinary map keys, not a\ntoken that requires callers to use `read-state`. A native embedder exposes\nthe equivalent immutable named-field state and explicit snapshot replacement;\nit need not implement Clojure records, but must preserve their transition\nsemantics. The `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` ABI (§E.3) serializes these snapshots\nand checked value references. Treat linear memory as a private execution\ncache. The guest-agnostic synchronization protocol across every host/guest\nseam is:\n\n1. **Public entry / host-to-guest:** `step`, `run`, and restore accept the supplied state map as authoritative. Import the guest's declared machine-state fields (for the register machine's eight-field instance — `:pc`, `:registers`, `:frames`, `:continuation`, `:store-of`, `:value`, `:halted?`, and `:blocked?` — see [`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.1), plus the current code layout and engine state. W1 imports unconditionally. A later cache may skip only after proving all relevant keys agree with its last materialized snapshot; a retained private token alone is not proof. In particular, externally `assoc`-modified state (such as `(assoc parked :pc 2 :halted? false)`) must execute the requested state. Import a copied or privately owned active state shadow so running an old or externally modified map never mutates another returned map.\n2. **Guest-to-host:** before every state-consuming `engine/*` call, including `handle-effect`, pinning, collection, scheduler, park/resume and module work, materialize all of the guest's declared execution fields onto the VM record. Materialize them also at every public `step`, `run`, restore or inspection return. Keep the record's protocol implementations so `module/gc-roots` can inspect the current map. Dense memory synchronization without map synchronization is insufficient.\n3. **Host completion:** the returned host state is the new authority, including its execution fields and every extension key. Apply only the current operation's specified post-service changes to that returned state, then reimport it into memory. Never overlay a pre-service header onto it. In particular, `handle-effect` sets `:value` even for nonblocking effects; preserve that value, resource/heap/GC updates, stores, counters, queues and custody fields. A plain non-tail primitive call, by contrast, only updates the guest's destination/program counter in the kernel and does not automatically set `:value`.\n4. **Seam site coordinate:** before a host service, the map contains the instruction's absolute site coordinate (`:pc`), unless the source transition explicitly advances it first (such as parking in the FFI path). Capture the boundary payload at the site; apply the operation's coordinate update exactly once after service completion. Mailbox state does not count as a completed guest instruction.\n\nThis synchronization barrier is also a GC safety requirement: `:cell/new` may\ncollect *inside* `handle-effect`, before the adapter receives its result. At\nthat moment map-based execution fields (`:value` and the guest's active\nregisters/frames/continuation) must already be current. Reuse the engine's\nexact logical heap tracing rules; arena retention of a host value cannot\nsubstitute for marking the heap cells that value references.\n\nThe program store is logically an immutable map, usually name-keyed but **not\nrestricted to names**: `:store-get`/`:store-put` accept `:data` keys,\nincluding keywords and numbers in the corpus. Its linear-memory\nrepresentation is a root `ValueRef` to the embedder's persistent map, not a\nsecond wasm hash map; the map and updates are addressable through ABI value\ndefinitions/state messages on every runtime. All writes go through\n`engine/put-active` → `engine/store-put`. Non-nil `store-of` selects only\nthat module's store, with no ambient fallback. Private `:resources` and heap\nare distinct from the program store.\n\nThe engine state roots retained across linear memory and host synchronization\ninclude `ValueRef`s/tokens for `store`, `module-stores`, `free-env`,\n`primitives`, `modules`, `wait-set`, `ready-queue`, `parked`, `resources`,\n`heap`/`gc`, `owner`, `origin`/`ancestry`, `id-counter`, `:yin.k/gate`,\n`:yin.k/issued`, and `:yin.k/closes`, preserving all other extension keys as\nwell. The actual issue-counter key is `:yin.k/issued`, distinct from\n`id-counter`; do not invent `:yin.k/next-issue`. These are state/arena\nretention roots, not an expansion of the engine's logical heap-GC root set.\nThe full existing state map is authoritative for engine extension keys.\n\n### E.2 The value arena, handle discipline, and three encoding domains\n\n#### E.2.1 Task-owned value arena, `i32` handles, and the native `handle↔Value` facade\n\nGuest execution operates on **`i32` `ValueRef` handles**, not NaN boxing and\nnot `externref` registers. Reserve `0 = nil`, `1 = false`, `2 = true`; all\nother values use checked arena indices. Every integer, float, string, symbol,\nkeyword, collection, primitive, closure, continuation, and sealed\nstream/cursor reference is an arena entry. Numeric computation initially\nremains in embedder primitive services: the CLJS adapter uses existing\nimplementations, and the native service implements the same declared\nprimitive profiles, integer carriers, float behavior, equality and metadata\nsemantics. W0 codec/profile fixtures prohibit substituting Rust or JS default\nnumeric/coercion rules. Zero, empty strings, and empty collections remain\ntruthy: only references `0` and `1` are false.\n\nEach embedder owns an explicit per-task arena, not a process global. Linear\nmemory contains a parallel descriptor table: kind, generation, opaque\nhost-slot and optional machine-record offset. The slot is a task-local\nnumeric identity, never a JS object address or native pointer. All service\nexchanges serialize plain-data messages in linear memory under the\n`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` ABI (§E.3), including in Node; there is no privileged\ndirect-object fast path. Known immutable arena values may be referenced by\nchecked task-local ids in those messages, with definitions transmitted on\nfirst use.\n\nCLJS reconstructs its actual values and uses `values/closure`,\n`values/continuation` and typed effects. The native service uses the Rust\ndesign's `yin-value` `Value` consumed by `yin-kernel`: the fixed 16-byte\n`#[repr(C, u8)]` enum with safe-Rust `Rc` ownership, explicitly not NaN-boxed\n(K-D2; `docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:324–392, 1238–1244`). Its equality,\nhashing and opaque printing follow that shared implementation. Trusted kernel\nconstructors mint closure/continuation identities; literal maps remain data\nregardless of keys. Native primitive/effect capabilities are supplied\nexplicitly by the composition, never encoded as function pointers.\nPointer/handle equality is not guest equality. Round-trip tests include\nmetadata, keyword versus symbol, exact integers, float bit representations,\nnested collections and trusted wrappers.\n\nStream and cursor *references* are sealed plain data, not host types in\n`yin.vm.values`. Their underlying stream handles and mutable cursor resources\nstay in `:resources`. `:make-stream`, primitive functions, and bridge\nhandlers are composition services, never serialized guest values. Any\nreturned effect must pass `module/effect?` and `engine/check-callee-effect!`;\na map shaped like an effect is not dispatched.\n\nThe native **`handle↔Value` facade** is an explicit adapter layer, not a\nreplacement `Value` representation. Each task owns an arena of those\n`yin-value` `Value`s; `i32` handles index its entries, with `0`/`1`/`2`\nresolving to `Nil`/`false`/`true`. Lookup checks task ownership, bounds, kind\nand generation before calling safe kernel/engine APIs. Allocation/retention\nclones `Value`s through their safe `Clone`/`Drop` implementation; arena\nrelease drops them there. Neither Rust pointers, enum bytes, `Rc`\npayloads/counts nor a borrowed `Machine` address appear in wasm memory or\nmessages. Generation/no-reuse rules are exactly §E.2.3. Machine-local\n`Value`s/arenas are not `Send`/`Sync`; immutable admitted `Image`s retain\nplain owned literals and `Arc` sharing, materializing constants per task\n(`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:394–432, 553–563`). Stream/cursor/cell\nreferences remain ordinary sealed `Map` `Value`s, with actual handles in\ncomposition resources.\n\nThe facade inherits the determinism rules of §E.3.3 (r5 §4.1.1) **unchanged**:\norder by recursively expanded canonical encoded value bytes before handle\ncompression, never arena indices; canonical float64 NaN `0x7ff8000000000000`\nwhile preserving integral-float carriers and `-0.0`; and one outer TLV\npayload-length framing with no alternative collection counts. It does not\ncopy Rust discriminants onto the wire. Rust's `Double` implements the wire's\nFloat64 semantic class; “Float64 variant” denotes that class, not a newly\ninvented native enum variant. The three encoding domains stay separate.\n\n#### E.2.2 Three encoding domains and mandatory native hashing\n\nThe shared Rust crates must implement **three distinct codecs**, not\nsubstitute the convenient ABI transport encoding for repository identity\nbytes:\n\n| Domain | Existing authority / exact obligation | Observable products |\n|---|---|---|\n| Private import messages | §E.3.3 (r5 §4.1.1) TLV codec, reference expansion, framing and NaN rules | ABI request/response bytes only; not code identities or seals. |\n| Executable register scalar/image encoding | `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc` `scalar-class`, `encode-scalar`, and `framed`; `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc` `encode-register-image`, `descriptor-hash`, `register-hash` | R for empty/base/attached/composed images, layout slice checks and continuation self-consistency. |\n| Jing canonical content encoding | `src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc` `encode`/`decode` and its existing corpus; `src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc` `canonical-bytes`, `content-hash`, `segment-key` | Owner tags, reference seals, primitive-profile addresses, module/semantic addresses and existing content admission. |\n\nPort executable framing exactly: class tags and eight-hex-digit **UTF-8 byte\nlength** prefix content; some content is hex-encoded numeric bits, while\nstring content is the actual string. `jing/blake3` hashes the UTF-8 bytes of\nthe resulting **string**, not hex-decoded bytes. R is BLAKE3 of the string\nconcatenation of descriptor-hash text and encoded-register-image text\n(`debruijn_register_code.cljc:258–271`). Do not replace it with a CBOR hash\nor hash the ABI node bytes. Preserve descriptor material, body order, tuple\noperand order, exact scalar classification, no NFC, unsupported-value rules,\nnumeric endian rules and canonical-key collision refusals\n(`debruijn_code.cljc:198–203, 224–242, 350–378, 410–465`). The native backend\nmatches the admitted CLJS scalar profile where that host rejects JVM-only\nscalar classes; broader Jing integer support does not silently widen\nregister-image admission.\n\nPort Jing's actual canonical CBOR profile, not a generic library's defaults:\nkeyword/symbol/list tags, exact integer/bignum, float64 carriers,\ndecimal/rational forms, collection ordering, metadata stripping/validation,\nequality-collapse and duplicate checks, depth limits, surrogate refusals and\ndecode/re-encode identity. The private ABI may carry a lone UTF-16 surrogate\nfor in-machine fidelity; that does not make it hashable by either repository\ncodec. Keep that domain distinction and the existing qualified refusal.\n`jing/content-hash` digests canonical CBOR bytes directly and returns\nlowercase hex; `jing/segment-key` applies the exact\nnamespace/algorithm/digest spelling. The default here is BLAKE3; any\nadvertised alternative algorithm must reproduce the repository registry's\nbehavior rather than aliasing BLAKE3.\n\nRequired formulas are the actual inputs in source: owner = content-hash of\n`[:yin.k/owner secret]` (nil secret → nil owner); seal = content-hash of\n`[:yin.k/seal secret kind id]` with `issue-ref`'s missing-secret refusal;\nprimitive profile = content-hash of the exact description in\n`vm/primitive-profile`, followed by the `:yin.k.pp/blake3-…` keyword\nconstruction; module address = segment-key of the manifest. Preserve A's\nexisting canonical construction as well. Equal test secrets, identities and\ninput data must yield **bit-identical** R, owner, seal, profile and address\nacross CLJS and Rust. Do not normalize these fields away or let the native\nservice trust supplied digest strings instead of recomputing them.\n\nAssign executable scalar encoding to `yin-scalar`, image/descriptor/R and\nadmission to `yin-image`, Clojure values/equality to `yin-value`, primitive\nprofiles to `yin-prims` with engine composition, and Jing payload/content\nserialization to `yin-wire` using the canonical Jing implementation. The\nfacade alone owns private ABI framing/reference expansion; it calls these\ncrates for identity bytes and hashes. These are the crate responsibilities in\n`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1136–1145`, extended for the current\ncontracts; never duplicate codec ports inside each runtime wrapper. W0\nports/verifies these codecs and hashing primitives before native execution\ngates. Reuse `test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.json` and its README/errata plus\n`test/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc`,\n`test/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc`,\n`test/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc` and\n`test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code_test.cljc`; do not regenerate existing\ngoldens. Add cross-embedder vectors for descriptor-hash/R (including empty and\nconcatenated images), exact encoded bytes, owner/seal/profile inputs and\nmanifest addresses. W1 verifies load/primitive profiles, W2 recomputes\ncontinuation hashes, W3 checks every minted reference seal, and W4 checks\ncompose/attach/layout/module addresses and received slices. These are native\nservice obligations independent of the ABI codec implementation.\n\n#### E.2.3 Arena collection, GC root discipline, and snapshot isolation\n\nNo collection runs while a guest holds unreported local values. Initially run\narena collection only after full materialization at an exit; engine heap\ncollection may run inside a host service after the mandatory map barrier in\n§E.1; later dirty-slot spilling can replace write-through with a separate\nGC/inspection barrier. Mark the guest's materialized execution fields (for\nregister machines, see [`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md)\n§10.1.2), `value`, `parked`/`wait-set`/`ready-queue` entries, `store`,\n`module-stores`, engine heap and pinned/outgoing values, constants, and\npending mailbox arguments. Traverse trusted wrappers via `values/payload` and\npreserve the kernel's `module/gc-children` implementation and its treatment\nof code. Integrate `engine/pin-refs` for arguments leaving through FFI. Keep\nembedder arena ownership and the existing logical heap GC distinct, with\nexplicit roots connecting them. Reclaim only unreachable implementation\nstorage, not stream history or persistent datoms. Avoid handle reuse\ninitially; later reuse needs generations checked at every host import.\n\nExternal state inspection produces immutable host snapshots. Resuming one\nsnapshot cannot mutate another snapshot's frames, values, or state fields.\nPersistent snapshots share immutable arena objects, never a mutable guest\nexecution buffer. The first implementation may copy the active state shadow\nat every public IVM transition; optimize only after snapshot-alias tests pass.\n\n### E.3 Service protocol: `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`\n\n*(Owner amendment note, 2026-10-19: By explicit owner ruling, the service\nABI is named `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`, renamed from `yin.register.host.v1` in\nr5 §4.1.1, and its canonical tables live in this section. Other\nspecifications reference these tables rather than duplicating them.)*\n\nThe following names define the **engine host implementation ABI**\n(`yin.vm.engine.host.v1`, r5 §4.1.1), not repository functions or a guest\ninstruction-set schema change. All runtimes supply identical imports. The\nshared runtime module imports `memory` (one wasm32, non-shared memory),\n`functions` (one original function table), and\n**`exchange(control_ptr: i32) -> i32`** from module name\n`yin.vm.engine.host.v1`. Generated component modules import the same\nmemory/table and the service entry as needed, plus declared immutable `i32`\nbase-offset words. No import/export passes a JS value, `externref`, Rust\npointer, string object, multi-value result or `i64` through a host-call\nsignature. `i64` pcs/numbers are bytes in memory; core `i64` computation\ninside wasm is allowed.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
