{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '641,941p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md\"","aggregated_output":"#### E.3.1 Offsets and ownership\n\nReserve bytes `0–255` for ABI control, initially zeroed; `control_ptr` is `0`\nfor v1. At offsets `+0`/`+4`/`+8`/`+12` store little-endian `u32` magic\n`0x31524e59`, major `1`, minor `0`, header length `64`. Offsets `+16`/`+20`\nhold request pointer/length; `+24`/`+28` response pointer/capacity; `+32`\nResponse written length; `+36` required response length; `+40`/`+44` exchange\nsequence low/high; `+48` transport status; `+52` flags (zero in v1);\n`+56`/`+60` reserved zero. Initial request region is `[256, 32768)`, response\nregion `[32768, 65536)`; task allocation starts at `65536`. For larger\nmessages, allocate checked disjoint buffers in the same memory and update the\ndescriptor; these initial sizes are not semantic limits. Neither request nor\nresponse may overlap control or machine records. W0 records maximum\nmessage/depth/arena budgets and exact qualified resource-limit results.\n\n| Control offset (bytes) | Type | Field |\n|---|---|---|\n| `+0` | `u32` LE | Magic `0x31524e59` |\n| `+4` | `u32` LE | ABI major version (`1`) |\n| `+8` | `u32` LE | ABI minor version (`0`) |\n| `+12` | `u32` LE | Header byte length (`64`) |\n| `+16` | `u32` LE | Request buffer pointer |\n| `+20` | `u32` LE | Request byte length |\n| `+24` | `u32` LE | Response buffer pointer |\n| `+28` | `u32` LE | Response buffer capacity |\n| `+32` | `u32` LE | Response written byte length |\n| `+36` | `u32` LE | Required response byte length (on `NEED_SPACE`) |\n| `+40` / `+44` | `u32` LE × 2 | Exchange sequence number (low / high) |\n| `+48` | `u32` LE | Transport status (`0`–`4`) |\n| `+52` | `u32` LE | Flags (`0` in v1) |\n| `+56` / `+60` | `u32` LE × 2 | Reserved (`0`) |\n\nThe guest writes the complete request/header, then invokes `exchange`. The\nhost reads/copies and validates it, performs at most one transition, writes\nresponse bytes and result lengths/status, and returns the same status as\n`i32`. The host cannot grow memory or re-enter guest exports inside\n`exchange`; guest allocation/growth occurs between calls and all memory views\nmust be refreshed afterwards. The host must bounds-check unsigned\npointer+length sums without overflow. It may write only response/control\noutput fields, not mutate the guest's state shadow behind the guest's back.\nThe guest imports response state only after checking ABI/task/sequence and\ndecoding completely.\n\n#### E.3.2 Transport status values, execution statuses, and once-only response delivery\n\n**Transport status values:**\n- `0` `COMPLETE` (response available)\n- `1` `NEED_SPACE` (completed response retained, required length at `+36`)\n- `2` `BAD_MESSAGE` (malformed bytes/descriptor)\n- `3` `ABI_MISMATCH`\n- `4` `RESOURCE_LIMIT`\n\nVM execution status is a field of a successful decoded response: `TRANSFER`\n(continue trampoline), `HOST_SERVICE` (complete current instruction),\n`YIELDED` (budget), `BLOCKED`, `PARKED`, `HALTED`, `ERROR`. These statuses\nare private scheduling results; exported VM maps retain exact `:blocked?`,\n`:halted?`, `:value`, `wait-set`, `ready-queue`, and `parked` behavior.\n**Guest blocking is a normal return, never a trap and never `NEED_SPACE`.**\nQualified semantic refusals/errors are `ERROR` responses containing plain\ndiagnostic data, never host exceptions crossing the import. The CLJS public\nadapter raises its normal host exception only after decoding that result;\nnative public APIs return/raise the equivalent diagnostic. Unexpected wasm\nvalidation/memory traps remain implementation faults and never substitutes\nfor `:yin/blocked` or an FFI error envelope.\n\n**Exactly-once response delivery:** `task + exchange sequence` identifies one\nrequest; the host retains its response until the guest acknowledges it in the\nnext request. On `NEED_SPACE`, resize and issue a transport `fetch-response`\nfor that same sequence; do not replay the semantic action. Repeat fetch\nreturns the retained bytes. A repeated sequence with different request\ncontent is `BAD_MESSAGE`. An effect is never repeated merely because its\nencoded response did not fit. A future retry after process failure is not\nimplied by this in-memory guarantee; external effect correlation continues to\nuse the existing stream protocol.\n\n#### E.3.3 Plain-data TLV encoding, canonical ordering, and Float64/NaN rules\n\n**Plain-data encoding:** the private codec has one framing rule:\n`u8 tag | u32 payload-byte-length (LE) | payload`. The length excludes the\nfive-byte header. There are **no inner element counts or text code-unit\ncounts**. Parse exactly to the enclosing byte boundary; truncated nodes,\nexcess trailing bytes, arithmetic overflow, malformed arity and\nduplicate/colliding map keys or set elements are `BAD_MESSAGE`. A top-level\nmessage is exactly one node. The following table fixes v1 payloads;\nimplementations must not infer a format from their serializer defaults.\n\n| Tag | Payload (no inner count) |\n|---|---|\n| `0` / `1` / `2` | `Nil` / `false` / `true`: length zero. |\n| `3` | Exact integer: sign byte `0`/`1`, then minimal big-endian unsigned magnitude bytes. Zero is sign `0` with no magnitude; negative zero and leading-zero magnitudes are noncanonical. |\n| `4` | Float64: length exactly 8, IEEE-754 little-endian, with every NaN encoded as `00 00 00 00 00 00 f8 7f` (numeric bit pattern `0x7ff8000000000000`). |\n| `5` | String: only UTF-16LE code-unit bytes, even byte length; empty string has length zero. No prefix count or implicit terminator; lone surrogates round-trip in this private domain. |\n| `6` / `7` | Keyword / symbol: exactly two complete child nodes, namespace (`nil` or tag-5 string) and name (tag-5 string). Identifier validity follows the receiving value domain. |\n| `8` / `9` | Vector / list: zero or more complete child nodes, in original sequence order, ending exactly at outer byte boundary. |\n| `10` | Map: zero or more complete alternating key/value child nodes; odd child count is invalid. Canonical underlying-value order below is required. |\n| `11` | Set: zero or more complete child nodes, in canonical underlying-value order below. |\n| `12` | Metadata pair: exactly two child nodes, value then metadata. This private order does not replace Jing's distinct `[meta value]` wire convention. |\n| `13` | Checked task-local arena reference: length 8, `u32` id then `u32` generation, both LE. Never itself a value-order key. |\n\n**Ordering independent of interning:** define expanded canonical ABI bytes of\na plain value by recursively resolving every checked arena reference and\nencoding its value without tag `13`. Map keys and set elements sort by\nunsigned lexicographic comparison of these complete expanded bytes (shorter\nprefix first); this comparator is a private ABI rule, not Jing's ordering\nrule. Compute the order **before** choosing reference compression, including\nreferences nested inside compound keys. The actual emitted key/element may\nuse a reference once sorted; decoder resolves definitions and verifies the\nsame order. Reject duplicate canonical encodings and equality-collapsing\nmembers before constructing a host map/set. A missing/foreign reference is\n`BAD_MESSAGE`, never a numeric fallback sort key. Thus changing arena\nnumbers, preinterning or inline-versus-reference representation cannot change\nentry order.\n\nFor a trusted non-plain value used as a key/element, obtain a pure,\nnon-minting structural sort descriptor from the trusted value arena: kind\nplus owner/payload for Closure/Continuation, registered composition\ncapability identity/profile for a primitive, kind/payload for a typed effect.\nCanonicalize the descriptor's expanded value bytes under a distinct kind\nprefix, so a literal lookalike map cannot collide with a trusted type. It\nmust contain no pointer, arena id or iteration-dependent id. Descriptor\ngeneration must neither lift resources nor allocate cursor/stream ids. W0\nfixes descriptor tags alongside fixtures; a composition exposing an unnamed\nhost callable must supply a stable capability identity for this private\nordering (it remains non-portable on guest streams). Runtime-opaque values\nwithout such a descriptor cannot be advertised as supported ABI keys; this is\nan explicit capability admission failure, not unstable ordering or silent\ndata loss. Trusted-value order descriptors never authorize minting the value\nfrom ordinary guest data.\n\n**Float classification and round trips:** classify a CLJS Float64 carrier\nbefore testing `exact-integer?`, and use `yin.vm.integer.host/exact-integer?`\nsemantics for plain numeric inputs: plain JS safe integers are integers,\n**-0.0 is not**. Decode every tag-4 node into `dao.jing.cbor/float64` or\n`float64-from-bits` on CLJS so `1.0` and `+0.0` remain floating content; Rust\nretains a distinct Float64 variant. Preserve all non-NaN bits, especially\nnegative zero and infinities. Encode every NaN payload/sign as the one quiet\nNaN above; reject a noncanonical NaN wire node as `BAD_MESSAGE`. This matches\nthe existing canonical NaN conventions in the two repository codecs, without\ntreating ABI bytes as their hash inputs. A plain JS `Number` `1` without a\ncarrier cannot recover an earlier float distinction; fixtures must construct\nthe carrier explicitly. Exact integers beyond the safe range remain tag `3`\nwith exact magnitude. W0 tests `value→wire→value→wire` identity, including\ninteger `1` versus Float64 `1.0`, `±0.0`, infinities and multiple NaN\npayloads.\n\n#### E.3.4 Message envelope, task-local references, and required operations\n\nMessages are maps with ABI version, task id, sequence, acknowledgement,\noperation, input state revision, execution snapshot, argument\nvalues/references and newly defined arena values; responses carry sequence,\noutput revision, execution status, result, post-service state and newly\ndefined values or diagnostic. Every execution snapshot includes the guest's\ndeclared machine-state fields (for register machines:\n`pc`/`registers`/`frames`/`K`/`store-of`/`value`/`halted`/`blocked`; see\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.1), plus layout\ngeneration. Host-owned fields and immutable image/value data may be\nreferenced by previously acknowledged definitions; missing or foreign\ndefinitions refuse. Mutation is represented by a new state revision, not\nmutation of a saved snapshot. Unknown extension fields in named state are\npreserved. W0 emits golden request/response bytes and locks\nrequired-field/type checks for each operation; both sides use those fixtures,\nnot serializer defaults.\n\nArena-reference tag `13` is an internal task-scoped reference, not a portable\nvalue or a way for guest data to mint authority. Trusted capabilities\n(primitive, Closure, Continuation, typed effect, stream service) are\ninstalled by the composition or checked kernel constructors and recorded in\nthe trusted arena. Generic literal decoding cannot create them. Plain-data\ndescriptor maps in the service protocol may name a trusted constructor\noperation, but guest literal maps and FFI data never enter that operation\nchannel. The native value service implements the same owner/type/arity and\neffect-authorization checks as the reference. External continuation export\nresolves arena refs to plain payload values or refuses; it never exposes tag\n`13` as a guest transport representation. Park and resume records cross the\nhost boundary as plain data under the §3 entry key contract above.\n\n**Required operations:** handshake/capability-profile negotiation; value\ndefinition/lookup and trusted closure/continuation minting;\nfree-name/store/gensym services; primitive application with typed-effect\nauthorization and exact call-effect completion\n([`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md) §3.4); stream/effect/FFI transitions;\n`scheduler-round` and ready/parked restore; collection/pinning; admitted\nmodule attachment/spawn and closure/resource lift/lower. Each operation\nspecifies its reference CLJC transition and read/write field set in W0.\nRequests carry the materialized state before any service that can collect.\nPure guest body control, moves and guest closure calls stay in the guest; the\nservice is not allowed to “run the remaining program” to manufacture the\nexpected trace.\n\n### E.4 The embedder seam, single-engine-port rule, and deterministic traversal\n\n#### E.4.1 Host responsibilities, embedder targets, and `IVM` `step`/`run` contract\n\nThe existing CLJS engine is one conforming embedder of `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`;\na native service implementation shared by Wasmtime and Wasmer adapters is\nanother. Neither JS nor a browser is required to execute an emitted guest\nbinary. A Rust native embedder implements the ABI and semantic services\nwithout invoking Node, embedding a JS engine, or delegating transitions to a\nCLJS subprocess. Wasmtime is required beside Node from W1; Wasmer joins the\nrelease gate by W5. Browser embedding uses the same ABI. JVM and Dart remain\nsemantic reference lanes; binding wasm into their application APIs is\nseparate from the required standalone native embedders. WASI stays outside\nthe VM core and its imports; a WASI-based embedder may implement stream\ntransports/FFI below the same ABI.\n\nRead authorities (source symbols are semantic specifications for every\nembedder; calls to existing CLJC helpers mean direct reuse in CLJS and\nequivalently tested native transitions, not a requirement for native hosts to\nload Clojure): `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc`, `values.cljc`, `yin/vm.cljc`,\n`linker.cljc`, `semantic.cljc`, `semantic/derive.cljc`,\n`debruijn/register.cljc`, `debruijn_register_code.cljc`,\n`debruijn_register_effects.cljc`; the engine, semantic, linker and register\ndesign documents; and the semantic and register test corpora. In particular:\n- **A is the code identity**; R is the register image checksum. Do not publish wasm binaries as a new code identity, fetch code by a wasm checksum, or bypass admission.\n- Current `vm/machine-data?`, used by restore and payload validation, recursively accepts kernel-minted Closure/Continuation wrappers by their payloads, as well as exact-integer carriers. It is not identical to `plain-data?`. Arbitrary host functions/errors remain invalid. Preserve the predicate, not an approximate JSON test.\n- The current module-slice encoder in `engine/encoder` explicitly refuses reified and parked continuations with `:yin.k/status :yin.k/non-portable`, kind `:non-canonicalizable`. Preserve admissible native payloads and qualified refusals elsewhere; do not silently promise a new universal migration mechanism.\n\nThe host-facing adapter provides `create/load`, `attach`, `run`, `step`,\n`resume-parked(id, value)` and `restore-ready(base, entry, value)` over guest\ninstances. All entry/exit paths obey §E.1: callers can directly read or\n`assoc` the VM map keys, host calls see current map roots, and every\npost-host state is imported before guest execution continues. `read-state` is\na convenience, not a prerequisite for correct map contents.\n\nEach embedder supplies the same step/restore and scheduler contract over the\nABI. The CLJS adapter delegates to the shared engine; native adapters use the\nRust-kernel design's same `yin-kernel`/`yin-engine` services through the\nhandle/ABI facade, with no independent semantic service port. For the `step`\nmethod of `yin.vm/IVM`, run one guest instruction or the existing\n`scheduler-round` when inactive. For the `run` method of `yin.vm/IVM`, honor\n`engine/run-loop` ordering: active work first, then wait polling and ready\nqueue, then halt; wrap bridge dispatch with the equivalent of\n`ffi/maybe-run`. An optimized run chunk may execute many pure instructions,\nbut returns before every host service and exposes the same boundary state. It\nmust not poll early, reorder ready entries, or stop on a halt while ready\nwork remains. Preserve `halted-with-empty-queue?` rather than equating any\nprivate `HALTED` status with public completion.\n\nThe service mailbox is implementation glue, with no application callback. All\nadapters invoke primitives and sanctioned stream-handle operations through\nthe message contract. JS event handlers or native I/O completion handlers\nonly classify and append plain-data events; a separately driven pump polls\nthem and enters the adapter. External host effects remain request/response\nstreams. Neither a browser callback nor a native future resumes a retained\nwasm activation. Import execution completes synchronously with a response or\nblocked status; the import never waits on an external operation or holds a\nsuspended stack. Re-entry of the same task during an `exchange` is\nprohibited.\n\n#### E.4.2 Single-engine-port rule, fault containment, and Rust-kernel reconciliations\n\n**Single-engine-port rule:** the native service library **is** the\n`yin-value`, `yin-scalar`, `yin-image`, `yin-kernel`, `yin-prims`,\n`yin-engine` and `yin-wire` workspace designed at\n`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1134–1158`, extended with a handle/ABI\nfacade and thin Wasmtime/Wasmer wrappers. It is never a parallel port of\nguest execution, scheduler, validator, primitive semantics, custody, module\nstores, collection or FFI two-step. The facade decodes validated messages,\nresolves checked handles, invokes the shared transition functions and\nserializes their committed outcomes. It owns only transport/arena/runtime\nmechanics. The Node adapter decodes into existing CLJS records and calls\nexisting CLJC helpers. Native execution cannot be replay, a JS subprocess or\nsuccess-only stubs; missing required semantics fails the slice.\n\nThe “one transition implementation, two dispatchers” rule\n(`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:991–995, 1308–1311`) applies across these\nexecution strategies: CLJS kernel and CLJS wasm adapter reach the same CLJC\ntransition; Rust interpreter, native wasm facade and later LLVM helpers reach\nthe same Rust transition. Across CLJC and Rust there is necessarily a\nlanguage port, governed by one specification and one conformance kit—not\nliterally one callable binary. Within either host, a second service-specific\ntransition implementation is a defect. Generated service sites never\nreproduce call/return/restore/effect logic in a wrapper. Extract reusable\ntransition helpers in the owning crate if necessary, rather than dispatching\na second copy of the engine.\n\nPreserve check-then-commit atomicity and fault containment\n(`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:639–713`): refused transitions leave\nguest state unchanged; a panic is a kernel defect, caught before crossing the\nruntime boundary, poisons only its machine, and cannot become a success\npayload. ABI malformed-message refusals precede semantic work; retained\nresponses preserve once-only effect completion. Safe facade code uses checked\nslices and `Value` operations. Do not expand the `unsafe` surface beyond the\nRust design's explicit boundary crates (lines `1154–1158`); any unavoidable\nruntime binding belongs to a reviewed boundary, never the kernel/engine.\nImported service calls are internal execution plumbing; guest external\neffects still use stream emissions, not arbitrary synchronous host callbacks.\n\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md`](./yin.vm.rust-kernel.md) revision 2\nremains **design-only, not authorized for implementation** (status and\nsubordination at lines `3–35`, crates at `1134–1158`, decisions at\n`1234–1265`). The owner-authorized wasm plan explicitly **supersedes only the\nDEFERRED item “WASM as an artifact target”**\n(`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1294–1300`), with the following explicit\nreconciliations:\n- Current A identity and A→R admission govern over the older Rust topology and R-as-identity wording (§3, lines `282–318`; §7.5, lines `997–1011`).\n- Rust K-D2b uses `u32` indices and qualified host limits (lines `434–448, 1245–1246`); wasm keeps 64-bit **wire pc fields** ([`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md) §2.2), and the native facade checks every pc/operand/attachment against the Rust domain and declared limits before conversion or mutation, returning the same qualified `:host-limit`.\n- Rust phases exclude existing CLJC edits (lines `93–95, 1164–1165`). §E.4.3 below (r5 §4.1.2) is a separately identified **wasm-driven shared-reference stabilization prerequisite**, outside that K-phase restriction. Export a versioned extension of the common kit after that shared change, preserving original goldens/history.\n- Rust public `step` always interprets (lines `677–678`). The wasm backend's explicit one-instruction test/inspection mode is a separate dispatcher; optimized wasm run chunks obey the same boundary equivalence law ([`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md) §3.1.1).\n- The native wasm embedder needs Level-2 engine services in W2–W4 even though the Rust standalone-node roadmap leaves K5 last (§6.3, lines `748–781`; §10, lines `1170–1182`). Develop/reuse those **same crates** to the slice's required coverage; this schedules the necessary library subset for wasm, not the full K5 node or a new Rust network client.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '621,684p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md; sed -n '942,1136p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '621,684p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md; sed -n '942,1136p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md\"","aggregated_output":"### E.3 Service protocol: `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`\n\n*(Owner amendment note, 2026-10-19: By explicit owner ruling, the service\nABI is named `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`, renamed from `yin.register.host.v1` in\nr5 §4.1.1, and its canonical tables live in this section. Other\nspecifications reference these tables rather than duplicating them.)*\n\nThe following names define the **engine host implementation ABI**\n(`yin.vm.engine.host.v1`, r5 §4.1.1), not repository functions or a guest\ninstruction-set schema change. All runtimes supply identical imports. The\nshared runtime module imports `memory` (one wasm32, non-shared memory),\n`functions` (one original function table), and\n**`exchange(control_ptr: i32) -> i32`** from module name\n`yin.vm.engine.host.v1`. Generated component modules import the same\nmemory/table and the service entry as needed, plus declared immutable `i32`\nbase-offset words. No import/export passes a JS value, `externref`, Rust\npointer, string object, multi-value result or `i64` through a host-call\nsignature. `i64` pcs/numbers are bytes in memory; core `i64` computation\ninside wasm is allowed.\n\n#### E.3.1 Offsets and ownership\n\nReserve bytes `0–255` for ABI control, initially zeroed; `control_ptr` is `0`\nfor v1. At offsets `+0`/`+4`/`+8`/`+12` store little-endian `u32` magic\n`0x31524e59`, major `1`, minor `0`, header length `64`. Offsets `+16`/`+20`\nhold request pointer/length; `+24`/`+28` response pointer/capacity; `+32`\nResponse written length; `+36` required response length; `+40`/`+44` exchange\nsequence low/high; `+48` transport status; `+52` flags (zero in v1);\n`+56`/`+60` reserved zero. Initial request region is `[256, 32768)`, response\nregion `[32768, 65536)`; task allocation starts at `65536`. For larger\nmessages, allocate checked disjoint buffers in the same memory and update the\ndescriptor; these initial sizes are not semantic limits. Neither request nor\nresponse may overlap control or machine records. W0 records maximum\nmessage/depth/arena budgets and exact qualified resource-limit results.\n\n| Control offset (bytes) | Type | Field |\n|---|---|---|\n| `+0` | `u32` LE | Magic `0x31524e59` |\n| `+4` | `u32` LE | ABI major version (`1`) |\n| `+8` | `u32` LE | ABI minor version (`0`) |\n| `+12` | `u32` LE | Header byte length (`64`) |\n| `+16` | `u32` LE | Request buffer pointer |\n| `+20` | `u32` LE | Request byte length |\n| `+24` | `u32` LE | Response buffer pointer |\n| `+28` | `u32` LE | Response buffer capacity |\n| `+32` | `u32` LE | Response written byte length |\n| `+36` | `u32` LE | Required response byte length (on `NEED_SPACE`) |\n| `+40` / `+44` | `u32` LE × 2 | Exchange sequence number (low / high) |\n| `+48` | `u32` LE | Transport status (`0`–`4`) |\n| `+52` | `u32` LE | Flags (`0` in v1) |\n| `+56` / `+60` | `u32` LE × 2 | Reserved (`0`) |\n\nThe guest writes the complete request/header, then invokes `exchange`. The\nhost reads/copies and validates it, performs at most one transition, writes\nresponse bytes and result lengths/status, and returns the same status as\n`i32`. The host cannot grow memory or re-enter guest exports inside\n`exchange`; guest allocation/growth occurs between calls and all memory views\nmust be refreshed afterwards. The host must bounds-check unsigned\npointer+length sums without overflow. It may write only response/control\noutput fields, not mutate the guest's state shadow behind the guest's back.\nThe guest imports response state only after checking ABI/task/sequence and\ndecoding completely.\n\n#### E.3.2 Transport status values, execution statuses, and once-only response delivery\n#### E.4.3 Deterministic service traversal prerequisite\n\nSource verification found real unsorted map/set walks that can mint ids or\nchoose diagnostics. Rust must not reproduce them with its default map order,\nand canonicalizing only the final ABI bytes is too late. Introduce an\nexplicit **shared deterministic traversal policy in the CLJC helpers and the\nnative service**, then use that revised reference for wasm parity. This is a\nplanned semantic stabilization of previously host-order-dependent behavior,\nnot an already satisfied property of current source and not a wasm-only\npatch. The traversal stabilization is planned work, not an implemented\nproperty.\n\nFor currently unordered traversal sites use a pure order key: for ordinary\nmachine values, the fully expanded canonical ABI value bytes defined in\n§E.3.3 (r5 §4.1.1); for trusted values, their non-minting structural\ndescriptors. Sort maps by key and sets by element, using unsigned\nlexicographic comparison, never arena numbering, hash-table iteration or the\noutput of a resource-lifting encoder. Build keys for a collection before\ninvoking any id-minting traversal of that collection. Retain vector/list\norder, ordered exports, wait/ready queue order and first-occurrence\ndeduplication. Retain **existing explicit source orderings** such as\n`sort-by str` on cursor ids/module-store addresses and `sort-by key` on\nattached image identities, with cross-host fixtures for those comparators; do\nnot replace them with ABI order under the guise of fixing unordered maps.\n\n| Site (existing symbols in `engine.cljc` unless qualified) | Required shared traversal change / preserved order |\n|---|---|\n| `encoder`, `encode-store` | Sort raw literal-map/store keys before visiting key then value; sort sets before recursively encoding. Never sort the already encoded output: `cell-for!` has already minted `:yin.k/c-N` by then. Keep vector/list and explicit export sequence order. |\n| `markers` | Maintain pre-order tree walk; for maps visit values in sorted-key order (preserve the source's values-only walk), for sets sort elements, for vectors/lists preserve sequence order. No incidental host map order may determine first stream discovery. |\n| `lower-resources` | Preserve sorted used-cursor ids and the direct-stream-marker pass followed by cursor-derived streams; consume canonically traversed marker sequences, deduplicate by stream identity at first encounter, then mint gensym ids. Do not reorder queues or change aliasing of repeated cursor cells. |\n| `receive-module` | Keep its explicit sorted image attach order. Enumerate slice values and fresh-store values in sorted-key order before `lower-resources`; use the same order for bindings/store decoding and deterministic error selection. Preserve slice-before-fresh-store phase order and first-link-wins stores. |\n| `module/module-entries`, `module/list-modules` (`module.cljc`) | Walk registry child maps in sorted-key order and return top-level module names in that order. Missing-module `:available` vectors and registry consumers no longer inherit host map iteration. |\n| `resume-from-run-queue` | Keep FIFO entry selection and terminal-check ordering; sort resource-update keys before selecting the first non-keyword diagnostic. Preserve updates and merge semantics. |\n| `lift-slice`, `decode-store` and adjacent consumers | Preserve ordered export input; if a collection is a set, sort before visitation. Audit downstream unordered traversal for id minting, first-error choice or observable vectors, and route those paths through this policy. |\n\nThe helpers named above exist; the ordering policy/helper itself is proposed\nimplementation work. It must land with shared CLJC/native regression tests\nbefore the corresponding W3/W4 acceptance gate. This deliberately can change\nold host-specific `c-N` assignments, stream ids/seals, slice-entry vectors or\ndiagnostic order; record those deltas as the named prerequisite, never\nnormalize them out. A fixed CLJS-only historical map order is not a\ncross-runtime contract. Existing canonical R/Jing hash algorithms and frozen\nbyte corpora remain unchanged.\n\nW0 inventories unordered observation sites and freezes order-key fixtures. W3\nimplements resource-update diagnostic order and validates counters/seals; W4\ncompletes module lift/lower ordering and tests equal maps built with different\ninsertion histories, small and large maps spanning CLJS map representations,\nnested maps/sets, repeated stream/cursor refs, multi-store modules and\nmultiple invalid update keys. Compare exact minted ids, seals,\n`:yin.k/entries`, `:cells`, `:available`, counters and resource maps. A parity\nfailure here blocks the slice; it cannot be hidden as implementation-private\narena variation. The old Rust K-phase restriction on CLJC edits remains\ndistinct from this explicit wasm-driven shared-reference prerequisite: version\nthe kit after that stabilization and retain the original goldens/history, as\nspecified in §E.4.2 and [`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md) §1.1's Rust-authority\nreconciliation; do not silently choose Rust map order or normalize away\nchanged guest-visible ids.\n\n#### E.4.4 Stream, park, custody-gate, FFI two-step, and `spawn-module` host rules\n\n**Stream and park transitions:** Stream `make`/`cursor`/`close` finish\nwithout suspension under the current contract. `put`-full and `next`-blocked\ncreate per-site wait entries using the guest's site builders. Advance the\nguest site coordinate once, set `blocked` true / `halted` false / `value`\n`:yin/blocked`, and return to the scheduler. Explicit `park` instead produces\nthe parked record as `value` and sets `halted` true; it is not automatically\nblocked and has no `wait-set` entry. The scheduler may still have other ready\nwork. Resume from a ready entry uses `engine/resume-from-run-queue`: pop FIFO\nentry, validate `resource-updates` keys, merge updates into `:resources`,\nclear flags, and handle terminal wake diagnostics before adapter restore. The\nsame errors must occur whether a stream failed immediately or after blocking.\nExported entries contain no callbacks, raw stream handles, timers, or stale\nservice-mailbox data.\n\n**Custody gates:** Retain `:yin.k/gate`, `:yin.k/issued`, `:yin.k/closes`,\nentry issue numbers, and every extension key across synchronization and\npublic map edits. Use `vm/gate-mode` and existing engine/FFI/module handlers;\ndo not replace them with one generic “gate means error” switch:\n- Any gate suppresses `ffi/put-request` append and returns `full`, so FFI retains its request as a `request-sent` writer. Link misses likewise park as `link-request` without minting a response cursor or appending; already-resolved module and install-join branches still follow the handler's ordering.\n- Any gate suppresses autonomous link retry/response scans, FFI response polling, and `put`/`next` retries in `check-wait-set`. Gated install children are stamped/stepped only in `running` mode; ungated scheduling remains unchanged.\n- `running` stream cursor creation creates the sealed reference and unminted resource cell without a stream cursor call. `exporting`/`ended` immediate observation paths refuse through the existing gate checks.\n- `running` `put` uses the engine's deferred observation path and stamps a blocked writer with `:yin.k/issue`, advancing `:yin.k/issued` independently of `id-counter`. `running` blocked poll becomes `:reason :observe, :op :poll`, using the call-site `next` builder fallback; its application remains the driver's operation.\n- **Current source nuance:** `running` stream close queues a record in `:yin.k/closes`, increments the issue counter, returns `nil` and does not call the stream handle. `exporting`/`ended` close refuses.\n\n**FFI two-step, without duplicate sends:**\n1. `ffi/require-call-pair!` runs before allocating an id or parking. Read args in vector order, pin references, build the boundary payload, derive `ffi/call-id` from task identity and `park-id`, and park under that correlated id. Increment the shared `id-counter` once through park.\n2. Build `apply2/request` and invoke `ffi/put-request`. On `ok`, enqueue `response-wait-entry`. On `full`, enqueue payload plus `request-sent` true, `call-id`, `op`, `reason` `:put`, call-in stream id and the exact request datom. Other outcomes preserve the existing qualified error. Under any custody gate `put-request` returns `full` without appending; retain the request for the driver rather than retrying it autonomously.\n3. The ungated engine retries a full writer. Its wake means the request append completed: restore converts it to a response reader, strips stale wake/writer fields through `ffi/response-wait-entry`, and stays blocked. It must neither append the request again nor advance the guest beyond the pending result.\n4. On correlated response, terminal response-loss handling runs first, then `call-result` unwraps success/error, parked `call-id` is removed, and the guest destination or tail return receives the result exactly once. Use constants from `yin.vm` for reserved resources. Keep the bridge in the existing host interpreter; do not implement wasm imports named after arbitrary FFI operations.\n\n**`spawn-module` isolation and node-local artifact-cache fallback:**\n`spawn-module` creates a fresh task/memory/arena/owner/counter/resources\ncontext, with the composition options copied by the kernel; it never borrows\nthe parent's mutable execution shadow or per-module store. Under the shared\nartifact-cache policy (`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:997–1011, 1113–1122,\n1261–1263`; [`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md) §5;\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.3), compiled\nwasm binaries are disposable **node-local caches**, never `dao.jing` content\nidentities, refs, continuation fields or portable module payloads. A missing\nor dropped artifact falls back to the same host's interpreter at any\nmaterialized boundary, with no pending mutation or active guest stack.\n\n### E.5 Conformance method, guest-agnostic acceptance gates, toolchain pins, non-goals, risks, and open items\n\n*(Owner amendment note, 2026-10-19: By explicit owner ruling, the\nconformance **method** previously housed in `docs/design/yin.vm.conformance-kit.md`\n(`§1–§5`, `§8`) is folded into this section as the acceptance specification of\nthe engine execution host; the register-specific fixture schema and register\nW0–W6 / Rust K1–K5 lane gates live in\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) `§10.4–§10.5`.)*\n\n#### E.5.1 Oracle export, differential execution lanes, and trace comparison method\n\nThe K0/W0 conformance kit is the single **shared deliverable** of\n[`yin.vm.rust-kernel.md`](./yin.vm.rust-kernel.md), this specification\n(`§E.1–§E.5`), [`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md), and\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) (`§10`). It is\nexported from R4 and extended for wasm lowering, `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`\ntransport, and runtime conformance, not a second reference interpreter or a\nsecond kit (`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1185–1199`, equivalence law at\n`:805–831`). The execution specifications retain their respective\nauthorization boundaries; the planned LLVM lane remains behind K3 owner review\nbefore K4. W0–W6 are the wasm slices; K0–K5 are the Rust design's phases. No\nnew fixture semantics are introduced. For the register machine's exported\nfixture schema (canonical images, R, B0-normalized results/errors, format-level\nboundary traces/K depth/saved pairs, 14 validator refusal rules, descriptor/scalar\nvectors, diagnostic messages, refused-step atomicity, 7.3 tail-site table,\n`>65,535`-register, host-limit, and million-iteration tail fixtures), see\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) `§10.4`. If K0\nexports are not yet available, create that shared deliverable once; do not\nfabricate a wasm-only reference interpreter. Record the actual reference\nrevision and fixture version, including the explicit shared traversal\nstabilization delta (§E.4.3).\n\nOne manifest drives **three execution lanes: R4 CLJS reference, Rust\nkernel/interpreter, wasm**. Wasm runs the same bytes under Node and native\nruntimes as gated below; all use the same scripted inputs, scalar/host-limit\ndeclarations and expected refusals. ABI-specific probes extend K0 as a\ntransport test group without replacing format-level fixtures. Rust interpreter\ncoverage advances with each W slice; it cannot be omitted because native wasm\nhappens to pass. B0 results and complete boundary traces remain shared; wasm\nsingle-step traces add finer evidence, not a second oracle.\n\nBuild an explicit differential adapter rather than replacing the existing\ntests' private `register-run` vars by global mutation. Parameterize/extract\nshared fixtures when implementation is authorized. Each case receives\nindependent scripted streams with identical inputs, capability secret,\nprimitive registry, origin and counter setup. From W1 onward, compile each\nimage once and run those identical wasm bytes under **Node and Wasmtime**,\neach against interpreted register after every guest instruction and scheduler\nevent; Wasmer must also pass the complete suite by W5. Compare serialized ABI\ntraffic as well as full semantic traces; also compare semantic execution where\nthe existing A→R fragment permits it.\n\nUse `test/yin/vm/semantic/corpus.cljc` **programs and literal goldens**:\nproject AST to A, assert the literal vector/address, derive R, validate R/live\nsets, compile and instantiate wasm. These goldens specify syntax/address, not\na runnable environment or expected final value for every entry. Supply\ndeterministic `f`/`g`/`h`/`k`/`x`/`y`/`c`/`loop` bindings, stream fixtures,\nand genuine parked targets for resume cases; otherwise compare the same\nqualified refusal. Execute both conditional arms where applicable. Do not\nsilently skip resume/terminal/closure cases or relabel unsupported cases as\nsuccesses.\n\nUse all fixtures/assertions of `test/yin/vm/debruijn/register_test.cljc`,\nincluding hand images, B0/B2 parity, stream scripts, continuation corruption,\nand IVM behavior. B0 normalization is suitable for final cross-model values\nbut is insufficient for this backend: additionally compare raw register\npayload shapes, stores/module-stores, `pc`, frame order, `K`, counter, flags,\nwait/ready/parked entries and emitted request traces. Compare resources by\nauthenticated logical identity/cursor progress and stream emissions, not by\nhost object address. With identical secrets and inputs,\nhash/owner/seal/profile/address strings, guest-visible ids and slice ordering\nmust be bit-identical. Compare canonical expanded observable-state encodings,\nnot pointer layouts; private arena numbers may be renamed only where they\ncannot affect key ordering, id minting or guest state. Across independent\ntasks use the same explicit owner inputs; never erase owner checks. Test\nprimitive implementations and scripted transports exist in both native and\nCLJS compositions with the same declared profiles; a JS function fixture must\nget an equivalent native fixture, not disappear from the native run. Normalize\nonly private memory addresses, arena numbering and runtime object identities;\npreserve all guest values, errors, `pc`s, frames, snapshots, queues, counters,\nstore/heap/custody changes, resource progress and emitted request order.\nCompare failures by qualified rule/reason/outcome and relevant message\nassertions.\n\nThe planned LLVM lane uses this same kit under Rust §7.2: interpreted and\nnative runs compare boundary states, ordered emissions and refusal\ndiagnostics. Artifact-drop fallback continues interpreted from the same\nboundary. Wasm adds wasm→Rust-interpreter and wasm→CLJS-interpreter fallback\nfixtures. No backend forks the validator, live-set rules or payload\nvocabulary. K3 may measure wasm on the same harness; a wasm result does not\ncommission K4.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1137,1296p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md; cat docs/design/yang.wasm.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1137,1296p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md; cat docs/design/yang.wasm.md\"","aggregated_output":"#### E.5.2 Cross-embedder encoding, hash, and traversal determinism gates\n\nAcross every embedder (Node/CLJS, Wasmtime, Wasmer) and the shared Rust\ncrates, the conformance vectors enforce the three encoding domains (§E.2.2),\nthe private `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` ABI (§E.3), and the shared deterministic\ntraversal prerequisite (§E.4.3):\n- **Three-domain hash and byte parity (§E.2.2):** Equal test secrets,\n  identities, and inputs yield **bit-identical** R, owner, seal,\n  primitive-profile, and manifest addresses across CLJS and Rust. Reuse\n  `test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.json` and its README/errata plus\n  `test/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc`,\n  `test/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc`,\n  `test/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc`, and\n  `test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code_test.cljc` without regenerating existing\n  goldens; add cross-embedder vectors for `descriptor-hash`/R (including empty\n  and concatenated images), exact encoded bytes, owner/seal/profile inputs,\n  and manifest addresses. W1 verifies load/primitive profiles, W2 recomputes\n  continuation hashes, W3 checks every minted reference seal, and W4 checks\n  compose/attach/layout/module addresses and received slices.\n- **`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` TLV and ordering conformance (§E.3):** Map keys\n  and set elements sort by fully expanded canonical value bytes before handle\n  compression, never by arena-reference bytes; float64 NaN is\n  `0x7ff8000000000000`, with integral floats and `-0.0` preserved by their\n  carriers; each node has exactly one outer\n  `u8 tag | u32 payload-byte-length (LE) | payload` frame, with no inner\n  counts. Duplicate/colliding entries and malformed nodes refuse. Task-local\n  references and trusted descriptors do not confer portable value authority.\n  Native handles resolve through the safe shared `Value` facade, not a\n  separate codec/engine port.\n- **Deterministic service traversal gates (§E.4.3):** Identical minted\n  `:yin.k/c-N` cell ids, stream/cursor ids and seals, `:yin.k/entries`,\n  `:cells`, `:available` vectors, counters, resource maps, and\n  resource-update diagnostic order across equal maps built with different\n  insertion histories, small and large maps spanning CLJS map representations,\n  nested maps/sets, repeated stream/cursor refs, multi-store modules, and\n  multiple invalid update keys.\n\n#### E.5.3 W0 baseline toolchain pins, feature probes, and transport/snapshot gates\n\nDeliver/extend K0's single exported fixture kit and its CLJS,\nRust-interpreter, and wasm adapters, the core-profile feature probe, pinned\nassembler/validator, ABI codec/schema, and operation-by-operation conformance\nfixtures. The following are **exact baseline pins** for W0, not “latest”\nconstraints or a claim of an already working build:\n\n| Tool | Pin / scope | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Node | `25.6.1` | Existing `mise.toml` node pin; keep the repository selection. |\n| Clojure CLI / shadow-cljs | `1.12.4.1602` / `3.3.5` | Existing `mise.toml` and `deps.edn` selections. JVM emitter uses the repository Java 21 lane; record resolved vendor/build in the W0 manifest. |\n| WABT | `1.0.37` | [Release](https://github.com/WebAssembly/wabt/releases/tag/1.0.37); assemble and validate with an explicit baseline feature allowlist. |\n| Wasmtime native crate/runtime | `=38.0.0` | [Versioned embedding API](https://docs.rs/wasmtime/38.0.0/wasmtime/); required native runtime from W1. |\n| Wasmer native runtime | `6.0.1` | [Release](https://github.com/wasmerio/wasmer/releases/tag/v6.0.1); second wrapper and completion gate by W5. |\n| Rust / Cargo | `1.90.0` toolchain | [Release](https://blog.rust-lang.org/2025/09/18/Rust-1.90.0/); host service/wrapper builds, not guest wasm generation. |\n\nW0 must build these pins together, freeze transitive native dependencies in a\nlockfile, record checksums and target triples, and run the ABI smoke tests.\nThey are reproducibility starting points, not assertions that all engines'\nlater features work. If a compatibility or security issue requires a pin\nchange, document the exact replacement and rerun all baseline probes; no\nsilent floating version. Binaryen is absent from the baseline toolchain, and\nany later introduction needs its own pin/profile gate. Browser engine/version\nis recorded when its adapter is qualified; browser API convenience never\nexpands the baseline.\n\n**Feature probes:** Probe imported memory, byte-order/bounds behavior,\nsingle-result `i32` imports, scalar `i64` memory fields, mutable globals,\nactive segments, table installation/`call_indirect`, memory growth/view\nrefresh, and multiple image instances sharing one task memory. Explicitly\nreject every opcode/type outside the baseline allowlist, even when an engine\nenables extensions by default. Test same-binary import/export signatures on\nNode, Wasmtime and Wasmer with no WASI imports. Native custom imports require\nthe embedding harness; invoking a bare runtime CLI with no imports is not this\ntest. Freeze deterministic fixture manifests. No new guest semantics. Gate\nevery nonempty image before codegen. Separate native payload transport from\nlinker export capability.\n\n**W0 baseline, snapshot, codec, and transport acceptance:**\n- **Baseline and snapshot isolation:** All semantic goldens retain their\n  literal vector/address; all derived images retain R and exact live operands.\n  Existing register tests pass unchanged on the reference lane. Invalid\n  contract, Rule R, unknown opcode, cross-body target, bad live set and\n  empty-image cases have identical admission outcomes. Validate a minimal wasm\n  instance and explicit resource-limit diagnostics. Establish matching-layout\n  payload transport and qualified refusal for incompatible layouts. Freeze the\n  map/memory authority protocol (§E.1) and its byte-message equivalents,\n  including external `assoc`/snapshot replacement, old-state replay (resuming\n  one snapshot never mutates another snapshot's frames, values, or state\n  fields) and preservation of unknown engine extension keys; do not defer that\n  contract to performance tuning.\n- **Additional W0 codec acceptance:** Run all three codec domains against\n  frozen bytes and hashes before executing native register images. Include\n  map/set canonical collisions, metadata, numeric carriers, surrogate\n  rejection in repository codecs versus private ABI preservation, deep\n  compound referenced keys with permuted arena ids, canonical NaN and\n  float/integer distinction, and exact outer-only framing (odd text lengths,\n  odd map node count, truncated/extra children). Keep private message-codec\n  and repository hash failures separately diagnosable.\n- **W0 transport acceptance:** Force small/zero response capacity:\n  `COMPLETE→NEED_SPACE→fetch-response`, repeated fetch,\n  acknowledgement/release, wrong sequence/task, repeated sequence with changed\n  content, `BAD_MESSAGE`, `ABI_MISMATCH` and `RESOURCE_LIMIT`. Validate\n  bounds/overlap/overflow and confirm malformed/unsupported requests do not\n  execute a semantic transition. A counter-only test service proves that\n  retained responses do not re-execute the action. These tests run on Node and\n  native harnesses during W0, not first in W5.\n\n**W/K dependency map:** W0 consumes/extends K0; W1 shares K1 pure\nkernel/value/image work; W2 shares K2 payload/restore work plus the required\n`yin-engine` park subset; W3–W4 require the Level-2 library services\ndescribed in Rust §6.3, not a second engine. W5 is wasm release\nqualification, not certification of the full K5 standalone node. W6 can\ncontribute wasm measurements to K3's same corpus/harness; K4 still requires\nthe owner's review of K3\n(`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1167–1182, 1215–1228`). No dependency\nauthorizes unrelated implementation. Register-specific W0–W6 implementation\nslices, per-slice differential acceptance gates, and retained Rust K1–K5 /\nLLVM gates live in\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) `§10.5`.\n\n#### E.5.4 Guest-agnostic non-goals and risks\n\nNon-goals: no second Rust kernel/engine/value/codec port; no K4 LLVM\nimplementation, full K5 standalone node, new Rust network transport,\ncross-node artifact service or Rust `Rc`-layout exposure; no universal\ncross-model continuation conversion, new portable continuation schema, direct\narbitrary host FFI imports, WASI imports in the VM core, threads/shared\nmemory, WasmGC dependency, numeric NaN boxing, a mandatory standalone\nhost-free runtime for W5, or immediate JVM/Dart application bindings; no\npromise to execute malicious wasm from the network; no optimization before W5\ncorrectness.\n\nGuest-agnostic host risks (register-specific risks live in\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.6):\n- **Host boundary overhead.** A `ValueRef` implementation deliberately leaves semantic services in conforming embedders, with mandatory serialized messages even for CLJS. Measure in W6 whether compiled dispatch helps before moving collection/value/store services into wasm; the native host implementation needed for portability is already required by W1–W4.\n- **Persistent public state over mutable memory.** Snapshot aliasing and old-state retention are correctness concerns, not just GC details. Decide in W0 whether each public state owns a copy-on-write arena root or a copied active shadow; require old-state replay tests before optimizing.\n- **Host exceptions and partial effects.** Catch host failures at the adapter boundary for shaped diagnostics, then reproduce the existing public exception behavior. Do not roll back an already appended stream event or retry a completed effect. The mailbox needs a single completion/commit rule.\n- **Predicate drift.** `machine-data?`, value wrappers, integer carriers and effect authorization have evolved beyond old doc prose. Reuse the source helpers, and make ABI tests fail when their contracts change. External plain-data serialization and internal trusted-wrapper values are separate gates.\n- **Single-port dependency.** Native coverage depends on the shared Rust crates, including the substantial Level-2 engine subset needed before W3/W4. A faster parallel port is not a workaround. Keep one transition implementation per host language, one exported K0/W0 kit and explicit shared-reference versioning; duplicate dispatchers must not duplicate semantic code. The `Value` facade and snapshot barriers must preserve check-then-commit and `Rc` ownership while keeping handles task-local.\n- **Host coverage.** Node/CLJS and native Wasmtime/Wasmer are shipping targets under one pinned baseline ABI/profile. Browser and later JVM/Dart application integrations may have different resource limits and must rerun the same parity suite. Native semantic-service drift and ABI codec fidelity are the principal portability risks.\n\n#### E.5.5 Reference conventions and open items\n\nReference convention: `register.cljc` means\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc`; `engine`, `ffi`, `module`,\n`values`, and `debruijn_register_effects` filenames are under\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/`; `vm` means `src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc`; `apply2` is the alias\nfor `dao.stream.apply` at `src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc`. `module/IModuleKernel`\nand its `gc-roots` method are declared in `module.cljc`. `IVM` `step`/`run`\nare methods of `yin.vm/IVM`. `halted-with-empty-queue?`, `scheduler-round`,\n`check-wait-set`, `handle-close` and `collect` are `engine.cljc` functions.\nThe quoted restore/call helper names are existing source semantics, whereas\n`exchange`/`fetch-response`, ABI statuses and all native implementation entry\nnames are proposed protocol vocabulary. Rust-design line references retain\nr5's coordinates (`1–1334`).\n\n**Open items** (retained from r5 without resolution across this specification,\n[`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md), and\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) `§10.6`):\n- §E.3.4 (r5 §4.1.1) says guest closure calls stay compiled, while the single-port rule in §E.4.2 (r5 §4.1.1) requires service sites to reuse the owning host's call transition. The division between generated dispatch and shared transition work needs to be made concrete without creating a second semantic implementation (see also [`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md) `## Open items`).\n- W0 retains the transport-test notation `COMPLETE→NEED_SPACE→fetch-response` (§E.5.3). §E.3.2 (r5 §4.1.1) defines `COMPLETE` and `NEED_SPACE` as alternative `exchange` results; the notation's test sequencing needs clarification, not an ABI change.\n# yang.wasm: lowering register images to WebAssembly modules\n\nStatus: design, owner-authorized planning; not implemented.\n\nConsensus provenance (2026-10-18): gpt-6-astra, author; gemini-4-argon,\nreviewer; five review rounds, APPROVE on r5. Author thread:\n`01a14f30-ca56-7530-bb93-3a870eca7a7b`; reviewer thread:\n`f365ef1d-caa2-4379-8e96-51e64ca36af8`. This specification distills the\ncompiler and target-ABI half of the frozen r5 consensus: semantic\nfidelity, portability, determinism and Rust-kernel reconciliation. It\ndoes not report an implementation or runtime acceptance result.\n\n2026-10-19 decomposition, owner-ruling, and fold notes:\n1. **Decomposition and owner-directed fold (2026-10-19)**: Register-image-to-WebAssembly lowering and all 23\n   opcode lowerings live in this specification (`yang.wasm.md`). By explicit\n   owner ruling, `docs/design/yin.vm.register-wasm.md` and\n   `docs/design/yin.vm.conformance-kit.md` are retired and folded into the\n   existing architecture documents: the guest-agnostic execution host, the\n   canonical `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` service protocol, and the conformance\n   **method** live in [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) (`§E.1–§E.5`);\n   the register-machine wasm execution instance, register fixture schema, and\n   register W0–W6 / Rust K1–K5 lane gates live in\n   [`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) (`§10`).\n2. **Owner ruling (2026-10-19, ABI naming)**: Because the host-independent execution\n   contract belongs to `yin.vm.engine`, the service import ABI is renamed\n   `yin.register.host.v1` → `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`, and its canonical\n   protocol tables live in [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md)\n   `§E.3`. Section 4 below specifies the compiler's target-interface\n   binding to `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` and references those tables. Normative\n   content is otherwise unchanged.\n\n## Authorities\n\n- [datom.world.md](./datom.world.md): foundational axioms and invariants.\n- [yang.antlr.md](./yang.antlr.md): frontend compilation into the Universal AST.\n- [yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md): Universal AST content and projection discipline.\n- [yin.vm.semantic.md](./yin.vm.semantic.md): A identity, semantic execution and A→R derivation.\n- [yin.vm.debruijn.register.md](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md): register contract, live sets, sparse continuations, and (`§10`) the register-machine WebAssembly execution instance (the eight materialized register fields, sparse continuations on wasm, `register-restore`, register fixture schema, and register-specific W0–W6 / Rust K1–K5 gates).\n- [yin.vm.linker.md](./yin.vm.linker.md): admission, offset tables and module transport.\n- [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md): scheduler, effects, custody, and (`§E.1–§E.5`) the guest-agnostic execution host, `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` service protocol, and K0/W0 conformance method.\n- [yin.vm.rust-kernel.md](./yin.vm.rust-kernel.md): shared Rust crates, Value ownership, transition reuse and LLVM relationship.\n\nSource precedence and the explicit Rust-design reconciliations below are\nretained from r5.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Pipeline position, decision and source precedence\n\nPipeline position:\n\n    yang source --(yang.antlr)--> Universal AST --(project)--> semantic vector A\n        --(yin.vm.semantic/derive register-image)--> register image R\n        --(yang.wasm, this spec)--> WebAssembly module (disposable local artifact)\n\n**A is the only code identity.** R is the register image checksum. The\nemitted WebAssembly module is a derived, disposable local execution\nartifact with no identity in `dao.jing`, no content address, and no\npresence in continuation payloads or module manifests.\n\nImplement a **compiled register backend with resumable body functions and an explicit\ntrampoline**. Each declared body becomes a wasm function with one local per physical register.\nIntra-body control initially uses generated `br_table` dispatch; guest calls and returns update\nexplicit machine state and return to the trampoline. No guest continuation ever depends on a\nwasm activation. Target host services through the versioned plain-data linear-memory import ABI\n`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` (§4 below; canonical protocol tables in\n[`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3, r5 §4.1.1). The existing CLJS engine is one\nconforming embedder; a native service implementation shared by Wasmtime and Wasmer adapters is\nanother ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.4,\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10). Neither JS nor a browser is required to\nexecute the same emitted binary.\n\nThe toolchain is a JVM Clojure emitter producing deterministic WAT, assembled with WABT and\ninstantiated unchanged by Node/browser, Wasmtime or Wasmer. This is emission from register\nimages, not compilation of ClojureScript to wasm. W0 names exact toolchain pins\n([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5.3);\nthese are implementation acceptance targets, not tools claimed to be installed or tested here. A\nRust native embedder implements the ABI and semantic services without invoking Node, embedding a\nJS engine, or delegating transitions to a CLJS subprocess. Wasmtime is required beside Node from\nW1; Wasmer joins the release gate by W5. Browser embedding uses the same ABI. JVM and Dart\nremain semantic reference lanes; binding wasm into their application APIs is separate from the\nrequired standalone native embedders.\n\nThe mandatory feature profile is **core wasm 1.0 with mutable globals and the original function\ntable / `call_indirect` facility**: i32/i64/f32/f64, structured control, one non-shared wasm32\nmemory and one function table per module, single-result functions, active data/element segments.\nNo externref or first-class reference values, typed function references, `call_ref`,\n`return_call`, WasmGC, bulk-memory/table instructions, multi-value, multi-memory, memory64,\nSIMD, exceptions, threads, component model or JS string builtins are assumed. The baseline table\nelement kind used by `call_indirect` is not permission to emit the later reference-types\ninstruction set. Copy/clear uses scalar loops; no implicit `memory.copy`, `table.set` or\n`table.grow` instruction may slip in through optimization. Mutable globals need not cross the\nimport boundary; instance base coordinates can use immutable scalar imports.\n\nEvery extension requires a separate named profile, W0 validation/probe on all target engines and\nmatching cache key; the baseline artifact must continue to work without it. WABT/Binaryen\ndefaults are not the feature contract. Uneven native-runtime support for post-MVP features is\nthe reason for this deliberate cut, not an unresolved portability risk. WasmGC remains a\nnon-goal despite its inclusion in the September 2025 Wasm 3.0 standard; standardization does not\nrequire this backend to adopt it. [Wasm 3.0\nannouncement](https://webassembly.org/news/2025-09-17-wasm-3.0/).\n\nWASI stays outside the VM core and its imports. A WASI-based embedder may implement stream\ntransports/FFI below the same ABI; the generated program imports neither WASI nor\nruntime-specific system calls. Binaryen is optional after parity; no wasm-ld pipeline is\nrequired for generated guest code. Native embedder builds have their own pinned native\ntoolchain.\n\nRead authorities (source symbols are semantic specifications for every embedder; calls to\nexisting CLJC helpers below mean direct reuse in CLJS and equivalently tested native\ntransitions, not a requirement for native hosts to load Clojure):\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc`, `debruijn_register_code.cljc`,\n`debruijn_register_effects.cljc`, `semantic/derive.cljc`, `semantic.cljc`, `engine.cljc`,\n`values.cljc`, `yin/vm.cljc`, `linker.cljc`; the register, semantic, linker and engine design\ndocuments; and the semantic and register test corpora. In particular:\n\n- The register contract is version 4 / `\"r2\"`, with exactly 23 opcodes. Preserve the image, physical L/T allocation, instruction pcs, BLAKE3 checksum, and six in-band live operands. Do not allocate another guest register namespace.\n- The October amendments to the register and semantic designs make **A the code identity**. R is the register image checksum. Existing `:hash`, `:image`, and offset rows still use R for native compatibility. Do not publish wasm binaries as a new code identity, fetch code by a wasm checksum, or bypass A-to-R admission. Retain access to the Universal AST through existing provenance/addressing.\n- Older paragraphs in register §5.2 still put code-space fields on return frames and restore code from payloads. Linker §7.3 r6/r7 and the implementation supersede them: return frames contain no code space; restore never rewinds it. Engine documentation (`yin.vm.engine.md` §1.1, §2.1, §3, corrected 2026-10-19) also contained historical `:store-updates` wording; current ready entries update private `:resources` through `:resource-updates`.\n- Current `vm/machine-data?`, used by register restore and payload validation, recursively accepts kernel-minted Closure/Continuation wrappers by their payloads, as well as exact-integer carriers. It is not identical to `plain-data?`. Arbitrary host functions/errors remain invalid. Preserve the predicate, not an approximate JSON test.\n- The current module-slice encoder in `engine/encoder` explicitly refuses reified and parked continuations with `:yin.k/status :yin.k/non-portable`, kind `:non-canonicalizable`. Thus universal linker transport of every register continuation is not already implemented. This lane must support the existing native payload where admissible, and preserve qualified refusals elsewhere; it must not silently promise a new universal migration mechanism.\n\n### 1.1 Rust-kernel authority, scope and explicit reconciliations\n\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md`](./yin.vm.rust-kernel.md) revision 2 is an authority for\nthe native implementation: status and subordination at lines 3–35, crates at 1134–1158,\ndecisions at 1234–1265. It remains **design-only, not authorized for implementation**. This\nspecification, [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) (§E.1–§E.5), and\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) (§10) record the owner-authorized wasm plan;\nthey neither commission K4 LLVM nor the unrelated standalone Rust node/transports. Where native\nownership, Value, transition reuse, fault isolation and crate boundaries govern, this plan is\nsubordinate to that design. Crate/type names below are proposed by that document, not claims of\nimplemented Rust symbols.\n\nThis plan explicitly **supersedes only the DEFERRED item “WASM as an artifact target”**\n(`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1294–1300`). Its other deferred items and K3-owner-review\nprerequisite for K4 remain. The following narrower reconciliations prevent silent\ncontradictions:\n\n- Current A identity and A→R admission above govern over the older Rust topology and R-as-identity wording (§3, lines 282–318; §7.5, lines 997–1011). R remains the exact executable checksum/offset-row token. Neither backend creates an identity or bypasses canonical admission.\n- Rust K-D2b uses u32 indices and qualified host limits (lines 434–448, 1245–1246); wasm keeps 64-bit **wire pc fields**, not a promise of unrestricted native execution. The native facade checks every pc/operand/attachment against the Rust domain and declared limits before conversion or mutation and returns the same qualified `:host-limit`. W0 records the common accepted profile; tests outside it require declared refusals, never truncation or a second widened Rust Machine. Wide register tests above 65,535 still fit u32.\n- Rust phases exclude existing CLJC edits (lines 93–95, 1164–1165). The deterministic service traversal policy in [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.4.3 (r5 §4.1.2) is a separately identified **wasm-driven shared-reference stabilization prerequisite**, outside that K-phase restriction; it remains an explicit scoped amendment for implementation review, not permission for a second native ordering policy. Export a versioned extension of the common kit after that shared change, preserving original goldens/history. This specification changes no source.\n- Rust public `step` always interprets (lines 677–678). Keep that rule for the Rust interpreter lane. The wasm backend's explicit one-instruction test/inspection mode is a different dispatcher used to verify its generated execution; it does not silently replace Rust's public `step`. Optimized wasm run chunks obey the same boundary equivalence law.\n- The native wasm embedder needs Level-2 engine services in W2–W4 even though the Rust standalone-node roadmap leaves K5 last (§6.3, lines 748–781; §10, lines 1170–1182). Develop/reuse those **same crates** to the slice's required coverage ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.4.2); a Level-1 offload service cannot satisfy native standalone wasm parity. This schedules the necessary library subset for wasm, not the full K5 node or a new Rust network client.\n\nOwner question 1 (lines 1282–1284) and C1/A3 (lines 243–258, 1275–1277) align with the owner's\ninstruction to lower held register images: wasm is a kernel execution strategy over an admitted\nimage, not a replacement source emitter. Do not reopen that choice or infer authorization for\nLLVM. The node-local cache choice aligns with question 2; compile farms remain out of scope.\nWorker envelope choice and K3 commissioning threshold remain with the LLVM work; W0's declared\nscalar profile fixes only this lane's supported domain, not all future Rust profiles (questions\nat 1285–1292).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Emitted module contract and linear-memory layout\n\n### 2.1 Values in emitted code: i32 ValueRef locals\n\nChoose **i32 ValueRef locals**, not NaN boxing and not externref registers. Reserve 0 = nil, 1 =\nfalse, 2 = true; all other values use checked arena indices. Every integer, float, string,\nsymbol, keyword, collection, primitive, closure, continuation, and sealed stream/cursor\nreference is an arena entry. Numeric computation initially remains in embedder primitive\nservices: the CLJS adapter uses existing implementations, and the native service implements the\nsame declared primitive profiles, integer carriers, float behavior, equality and metadata\nsemantics. W0 codec/profile fixtures prohibit substituting Rust or JS default numeric/coercion\nrules. Zero, empty strings, and empty collections remain truthy: only references 0 and 1 are\nfalse.\n\nEach embedder owns an explicit per-task arena, not a process global. Linear memory contains a\nparallel descriptor table: kind, generation, opaque host-slot and optional machine-record\noffset. The slot is a task-local numeric identity, never a JS object address or native pointer.\nAll service exchanges serialize plain-data messages in linear memory under\n`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` (§4 below; [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3, r5 §4.1.1),\nincluding in Node; there is no privileged direct-object fast path. Known immutable arena values\nmay be referenced by checked task-local ids in those messages, with definitions transmitted on\nfirst use.\n\nUsing i32 handles avoids passing i64 values as JS numbers or requiring externref persistence. It\ncosts host crossings and arena indirection. This is acceptable for the first semantic\nimplementation: register instructions themselves contain no numeric arithmetic opcode. A later\nnative scalar fast path needs explicit numeric laws and benchmarks, not a change to the public\nimage contract.\n\nHost-side value reconstruction, the native `handle↔Value` facade over `yin-value`'s 16-byte\n`#[repr(C, u8)]` `Value` enum (`K-D2`), and the three mandatory encoding/hashing domains\n(`yin-scalar`, `yin-image`, `yin-wire`) are specified in\n[`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.2.1 and §E.2.2.\n\n### 2.2 Private linear-memory layout\n\nUse imported, non-shared wasm32 memory, little-endian and bounds checked. One task owns one\nmemory arena; all its compiled image instances import that arena. Separate tasks receive\nseparate arenas even when compiled Module objects are cached. A supplied coremem can implement\nthe same interface only with task isolation and allocation ownership; shared mutable guest state\nis not introduced.\n\n| Region / record | Contents and rules |\n|---|---|\n| Task header | ABI version, run status, absolute pc as unsigned 64-bit field, body id, active register count/pointer, lexical-chain pointer, return-stack pointer, store-of ValueRef, value ValueRef, host-state token, code-layout generation, scratch/request pointers. Pointer fields are u32. |\n| Body directory | Absolute start/end pcs, register/local counts, image row, compiled function slot, body-relative entry mapping. Append-only while attached. |\n| Active register shadow | Dense array of u32 ValueRefs of exactly the current body's `:registers` size. L slots are initialized from positional arguments; T slots start nil. Wasm locals cache this array while a body executes. |\n| Lexical frames | Immutable vector records: count followed by ValueRefs. Chain is an immutable outermost-first vector of frame pointers. Depth d indexes `count - 1 - d`, then position. |\n| Return stack | Immutable/persistent vector of return-record pointers, innermost last. Records contain site-pc, return-pc, frames pointer, sparse pairs pointer, live vector pointer, dest, optional store-of. No segment/hash/layout snapshot. |\n| Sparse snapshots | Count followed by sorted `(u32 register-index, u32 ValueRef)` pairs. Records do not alias mutable active-register storage. Only the verified live set is captured. |\n| Continuation record | Immutable snapshot metadata: format/hash/image ValueRefs, capture-time segment and optional images ValueRefs, site-pc, pc, frames, sparse regs/live, return stack, destination presence/index, resume mode, optional store-of. Engine/FFI transport fields remain losslessly represented in the host payload. |\n| Value descriptors | Kind/generation/host slot plus optional closure or continuation record pointer. Host slot contains the actual immutable value or trusted wrapper. |\n| Engine state roots | ValueRefs/tokens for store, module-stores, free-env, primitives, modules, wait-set, ready-queue, parked, resources, heap/gc, owner, origin/ancestry, id-counter, `:yin.k/gate`, `:yin.k/issued`, and `:yin.k/closes`. Preserve all other extension keys as well. The actual issue-counter key is `:yin.k/issued`, distinct from id-counter; do not invent `:yin.k/next-issue`. These are state/arena retention roots, not an expansion of the engine’s logical heap-GC root set. The full existing state map is authoritative for engine extension keys. |\n| Exit mailbox | Pending instruction site/op and argument refs, pending result/error ref, completion kind. Private implementation state, never a portable continuation. |\n\nPcs use 64-bit fields so wasm32 pointers do not silently narrow the existing layout domain (up\nto `2^52-1` in `compose`). Functions use small body-relative i32 dispatch indices after checked\nresolution. Reject resource/address exhaustion with a qualified backend diagnostic before\nallocation/attach; never wrap a pc, handle, or byte offset. The counter remains the existing\nhost integer value and advances only through engine operations.\n\nThe program store is logically an immutable map, usually name-keyed but **not restricted to\nnames**: `:store-get`/`:store-put` accept `:data` keys, including keywords and numbers in the\ncorpus. Its linear-memory representation is a root ValueRef to the embedder’s persistent map,\nnot a second wasm hash map; the map and updates are addressable through ABI value\ndefinitions/state messages on every runtime. All writes go through `engine/put-active` →\n`engine/store-put`. Non-nil store-of selects only that module's store, with no ambient fallback.\nPrivate resources and heap are distinct from program store.\n\nThe guest-agnostic machine-state-as-data synchronization barrier is specified in\n[`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.1 and §E.2.3; the register machine's eight-field\ninstance and dense-register shadow spilling rules are specified in\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.1.1 and §10.1.2.\n\n### 2.3 Module imports, exports and deterministic emission\n\nThe instance exports conceptual `run(state, budget)`, `step(state)`, `read-state(state)` and\nvalidated state-install operations. The shared runtime module imports `memory` (one wasm32,\nnon-shared memory), `functions` (one original function table), and\n**`exchange(control_ptr: i32) -> i32`** from module name `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` (renamed from\n`yin.register.host.v1` by owner ruling on 2026-10-19; see §4 and\n[`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3). Generated component modules import the same\nmemory/table and the service entry as needed, plus declared immutable i32 base-offset words. No\nimport/export passes a JS value, externref, Rust pointer, string object, multi-value result or\ni64 through a host-call signature. i64 pcs/numbers are bytes in memory; core i64 computation\ninside wasm is allowed.\n\nUse finite status values: TRANSFER (continue trampoline), HOST_SERVICE (complete current\ninstruction), YIELDED (budget), BLOCKED, PARKED, HALTED, ERROR. These statuses are private\nscheduling results; exported VM maps retain exact `:blocked?`, `:halted?`, `:value`, wait-set,\nready-queue, and parked behavior. **Blocking is a normal return, never a trap.** Unexpected wasm\nvalidation/memory traps are backend faults and never substitutes for `:yin/blocked` or an FFI\nerror envelope.\n\nEmission is byte-deterministic: equal admitted register image, emitter/ABI version, and feature\nprofile yield byte-identical WAT and assembled `.wasm` binaries across runs and platforms.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Code generation strategy and all 23 opcode lowerings\n\n### 3.1 Choice among the alternatives\n\n| Strategy | Benefits | Costs / semantic obligations |\n|---|---|---|\n| Fully structured body functions with direct guest `call` | Small fast code for ordinary functions; conventional optimizer input | Captured continuations require every caller activation to be materialized and abandoned; arbitrary resume PCs need secondary entries; suspension cannot retain native stack frames. A shadow stack alone does not unwind native callers. |\n| One interpreter loop over instruction records | Small wasm binary, trivial append and pc resume | Runtime operand decoding and memory-indexed registers on every step; does not exploit known register operands as locals. Uses the same K0/W0 oracle; retained as a W6 optimization axis with services moved into wasm if serialized crossing cost dominates. |\n| **Compiled body dispatch plus trampoline** | Static register locals and operands, arbitrary entry PCs, appendable bodies, constant native stack depth, explicit continuations | More generated code than interpreter; indirect dispatch and spills at transfers; per-body branch tables may be large. |\n\nChoose the third. A body function has conceptual signature `(state-ptr i32, entry-index i32,\nbudget i32) -> status i32`; updated budget/state lives in the header. All bodies share the same\nsignature for a function table. The trampoline resolves absolute pc, calls one body function,\nthen loops on transfer. A body **never invokes a guest callee directly**. Guest call saves a\nreturn frame (unless tail), installs callee state, and returns TRANSFER. Return installs caller\nstate and returns TRANSFER; final return returns HALTED. The trampoline's wasm `call_indirect`\nis an implementation dispatch, not the guest call stack. On HOST_SERVICE the body has returned;\na shared runtime service thunk serializes the mailbox and invokes the `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`\nimport ABI (§4; [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3). It decodes the completed response\nor returns the blocked/error status. No guest activation remains live across an asynchronous\nwait. The same thunk and body binaries run on each embedder.\n\nConsequently flattening is exact and incremental: every pending guest call is already a sparse\nmemory record before its callee runs. Capturing K copies/shares those immutable records;\nabortive application replaces the current chain and returns from the one active body. No stack\nwalk, Asyncify, native-stack copying, wasm exception unwinding, or retained JS Promise\ncontinuation is needed.\n\n`return_call` is **not required or emitted**. Tail guest calls retain K unchanged and replace\nthe active lexical chain/register file. This gives bounded native stack depth on engines without\nthe tail-call extension. A later direct-call optimization may be allowed only for a proven\nnoncapturing, nonsuspending region with a deoptimization protocol; it is not part of this plan.\n\n### 3.1.1 LLVM sibling and shared equivalence law\n\nThe wasm path and Rust K4 LLVM path are sibling execution strategies under\n`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:805–831` (§7.2): from equal reachable boundary states they\nreach equal later states, ordered effects and refusal diagnostics. Both use body functions,\nentry-pc dispatch, live sets as spill maps and data guest calls; their native control\ninstructions differ, their guest semantics do not (§7.3, lines 833–918). W0’s shared kit tests\nall lanes; neither translator forks the validator, liveness rules, payload schema or semantic\nservice implementation. An artifact may be discarded at a materialized boundary and execution\ncontinued by the owning interpreter from the same state. Add wasm→Rust interpreter and wasm→CLJS\ninterpreter fallback fixtures; no artifact field may be needed to restore.\n\n### 3.2 Structuring algorithm\n\n1. Admit the image with the exact contract check and `register-image-defect`. Preserve body ranges and the original source instruction vector as metadata. Reuse `body-liveness`/`live-slot-index`; do not derive live sets from the optimized wasm graph.\n2. Build each body's CFG using the existing successor rules: conditional two-way edge; jump target; no successor for return/halt/resume or tail call; ordinary call and other operations resume at pc+1. Jumps must remain inside their body. Do not assume a hand-authored valid image is the structured syntax emitted by `derive`.\n3. For W1, make every instruction an entry case. Load locals from shadow, enter a wasm `loop` containing nested void `block`s and a `br_table` selecting the pc case. Each case consumes its operands into locals, leaves an empty operand stack, updates the next pc/case, then branches to dispatch or returns one status. All branch targets have the same empty result type. Functions return their status only at the common exit. Invalid entry takes a qualified fault path, not execution of arbitrary memory.\n4. For W6, merge straight-line instruction cases into basic blocks, retaining leaders at targets, body entries, all resume pcs, and every externally observable step/exit site. Preserve an entry path for each supported pc; debug one-step mode can retain instruction cases. General irreducible CFGs keep dispatch. A subsequent measured optimization can compute dominators/SCCs and structure single-entry regions as `block`/`loop`/`if`, retaining a local dispatcher for multi-entry SCCs. There is no need to ship a full relooper to get a correct backend.\n5. Check budget at instruction boundaries for bounded/debug execution. A budget yield materializes the **full** active state and returns YIELDED; it is not a new guest suspension site, has no invented sparse live operand, and is not serializable as an r2 continuation. `step` counts one guest instruction, even when several host service transitions implement it.\n\n### 3.3 All 23 opcode lowerings\n\nHere `r` means ValueRef local, `next` means pc+1, and “host service” means a synchronous\n`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` ABI message exchange ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3)\nimplementing the specified engine transition after returning from the body, not an application\ncallback or new external FFI path. The mailbox records the already-read operands so re-entry\nnever repeats an effect.\n\n| Opcode | Lowering and observable behavior |\n|---|---|\n| `:const rd value` | Load interned literal reference into rd; next. Preserve actual scalar/collection value and metadata. |\n| `:load-bound rd depth position` | Read frame chain at `count-1-depth`, then position. Preserve `:load-bound-depth` and `:load-bound-position` failures. Never substitute reserved L locals for the runtime chain. |\n| `:load-free rd name` | Host `engine/resolve-var` with free-env, active store, primitives, modules. Preserve lookup order, present-nil bindings, Rule R, and module resolution. Write rd; next. |\n| `:closure rd arity body-pc` | Mint owned Closure with type, arity, absolute body-pc, immutable captured frames, and optional store-of. Write rd; next. Captured frames outlive active body. |\n| `:move rd rs` | Read rs before writing rd; next. |\n| `:call rd fn-reg arg-regs tail? live` | Read operator and ordered arguments first. Use `engine/operator-kind`. Closure: nil-fill/drop extras to arity, append bound frame to captured chain, initialize L args and nil T slots; push exact sparse caller frame only for non-tail; set closure store-of and body pc; TRANSFER. Host fn: apply f to args. A typed effect first passes `engine/check-callee-effect!`, then follows the complete call-effect contract below (including blocked results and site builders). An ordinary result writes rd/next or executes return transition for tail. Continuation: `engine/operator-kind` checks ownership first, then `engine/continuation-argument` checks exactly one arg, then check format/image, abort current K and write back captured state; no external data gate on this internal path. |\n| `:branch-false cond target` | Branch only when nil/false; all other values next. Empty operand stack on either edge. |\n| `:jump target` | Set pc/case to the verified same-body target. |\n| `:return rs` | Read value. If K nonempty, pop frame, nil-initialize caller register vector, restore saved pairs and dest, frames and store-of, set value and return-pc; TRANSFER. Else set only value, halted true, store-of nil: keep pc at the executing return instruction and preserve active registers, frames, empty K and all other fields. The return-result restore case first changes pc as specified in [`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.2.2 (r5 §3.3). |\n| `:halt rs` | Set value, halted true, store-of nil. Does not pop K as return would; preserve pc at the halt instruction (no increment), active registers, frames, K, blocked flag and all other fields. |\n| `:store-get rd key` | Read literal key from active store, absent → nil; rd/next. |\n| `:store-put rd key value` | Write the **literal value operand**, not a source register, via put-active; return that value in rd; next. |\n| `:define rd name rs` | Read rs, put-active under literal name, write rd; next. Never resolve the definition operator. |\n| `:gensym rd prefix` | Engine gensym using the same task counter as stream/park ids; rd/next. |\n| `:stream-make rd capacity` | Mint authorized `:stream/make` effect; engine calls supplied make-stream. Preserve default-capacity handling; rd/next. Nonblocking under current contract. |\n| `:stream-put rd sr vr live` | Build site payload; typed put effect from read sr/vr. On success rd/result and next. On full enqueue exact writer wait entry, advance pc, BLOCKED. Other outcomes follow engine. |\n| `:stream-cursor rd sr` | Typed cursor effect, engine resource/cell handling, rd/next; nonblocking. |\n| `:stream-next rd cr live` | Build site payload; typed next effect. Immediate result → rd/next; blocked → reader wait entry, advance pc, BLOCKED; all other outcomes follow engine. |\n| `:stream-close rd sr` | Typed close effect and engine result, rd/next; nonblocking. |\n| `:current-continuation rd live` | Snapshot before writing rd at next pc, mint owned Continuation with `:type :reified-continuation`, write rd; next. Destination is excluded from saved live set. |\n| `:park rd live` | Snapshot and advance pc, call engine park-continuation. Adds id/type to parked record, increments counter, sets value to record and halted true. PARKED status; do not invent a stream wait. |\n| `:resume parked-id rs` | Literal keyword id and value from rs. Engine resume-continuation performs custody gate/missing-id checks and removes parked entry, then register restore. Abort current path; no next-pc fallthrough. |\n| `:ffi-call rd op arg-regs live` | Require call pair first; snapshot and read ordered args; pin outgoing refs; park under correlated call-id and advance pc; append request; enter reader wait or full writer wait. Always yields to protocol; no direct call of bridge handler from wasm. |\n\n### 3.4 Exact host-function effect completion\n\nFor `:call` whose `apply f args` result satisfies `module/effect?`, first use the state returned\nby `engine/check-callee-effect!`. Build `payload-of` at the original call site from the\nmaterialized state and relocated combined instruction. Tail calls use `:resume-mode\n:return-result`, `:dest nil`, and their verified empty live set; non-tail calls use\n`:write-result` and rd. Pass `{:park-entry-fns (park-entry-fns payload)}` to\n`engine/handle-effect`, with builders for `:stream/put`, `:stream/next`, and `:module/require`.\nThe module handler uses the require builder as fallback for `:module/try-require`; gated stream\npoll uses the next builder as fallback. Do not wire only the dedicated stream opcodes.\n\nGiven `{:state state :value value :blocked? blocked?}` from handle-effect:\n\n- Blocked: return `(assoc state :pc (inc site-pc))`, with no destination write and no return transition, even for a tail call. The payload retains the pending destination/return mode for later delivery.\n- Nonblocked tail: run `return-transition` on the **post-effect state** and value. Do not pre-increment pc: if K is empty, this immediate path halts at the call site. This differs observably from a blocked tail effect later restored at the payload’s next pc.\n- Nonblocked non-tail: set pc to next and write value into rd of `(:registers state)`, preserving the rest of the post-effect state, including `:value` set by handle-effect.\n\nNever reconstruct the result from the pre-effect wasm header: module attachment,\nstore/module-store writes, resources, heap/GC, counters and custody bookkeeping may have\nchanged. A non-effect host result follows the separate plain-result arm of `register.cljc`,\nincluding preservation of old `:value` on a non-tail call.\n\n### 3.5 Boundary continuation payload and internal continuation call\n\nAt a six-op boundary at p, export exactly the payload built by `effects/continuation-payload`:\n\n```clojure\n{:segment capture-time-segment\n :site-pc p :pc (inc p)\n :frames outermost-first-frame-vectors\n :regs [[live-index value] ...] :live [live-index ...]\n :continuation [return-frame ...]\n :dest rd-or-nil :resume-mode :write-result-or-return-result\n :format :yin.debruijn.register\n :hash capture-time-register-hash\n :image identity-of-row-containing-resume-pc\n ;; :images capture-time-offset-table when count > 1\n ;; :store-of module-address when present\n}\n```\n\nReturn-frame shape is `{:site-pc :return-pc :frames :regs :live :dest}` plus optional store-of.\nFrame order, sparse pair order, absent-vs-present optional fields, and keyword values are\npreserved. The `image-row` convention includes the one-past-last-row endpoint for address\nhelpers; that does not make it a valid write-result continuation pc: continuation-defect still\nrequires the site's and resume pc's body to agree.\n\nA `:call` of a trusted reified continuation uses the distinct internal path:\n`engine/operator-kind` ownership classification first, `engine/continuation-argument` arity\ncheck second, format/image membership third, then write-back; it deliberately skips\ncontinuation-defect/machine-data gates. For an unowned continuation with wrong arity, ownership\nfails first with `{:reason :foreign-value :kind :continuation}`, not `:continuation-arity` (and\nnot `:foreign-continuation`). A live primitive in an internal capture is valid and must not be\nrejected merely because it could not cross a wire. This is abortive, and applying a retained\ncapture more than once is allowed; do not mutate its saved records. Explicit parked-id\nconsumption is separate and removes that parked map entry.\n\nHost-side payload materialization, the 5-step `register-restore` algorithm, and cross-layout\ntransport refusals are specified in [`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.2.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Target interface specification: Import ABI `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`\n\n*(Owner amendment note, 2026-10-19: By explicit owner ruling, the service import ABI is named\n`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` — renamed from `yin.register.host.v1` in r5 §4.1.1 — and its canonical\nprotocol tables live in [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3. This section specifies the\ncompiler's target-interface binding and references those tables rather than duplicating them.)*\n\nEmitted WebAssembly modules target the guest-agnostic engine service protocol\n**`yin.vm.engine.host.v1`** defined in [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3 (r5 §4.1.1):\n\n1. **Module import bindings ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3):** The shared runtime module imports `memory` (one wasm32, non-shared memory), `functions` (one original function table), and **`exchange(control_ptr: i32) -> i32`** from module name `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`. Generated component modules import the same `memory`/`functions` and the service entry as needed, plus declared immutable `i32` base-offset words. No import or export passes a JS value, `externref`, Rust pointer, string object, multi-value result, or `i64` through a host-call signature.\n2. **Control-block layout and buffer ownership ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3.1):** Emitted service thunks address ABI control at bytes `0–255` (`control_ptr = 0` in v1, magic `0x31524e59` at `+0`, major `1` at `+4`, minor `0` at `+8`, header length `64` at `+12`, request pointer/length at `+16`/`+20`, response pointer/capacity at `+24`/`+28`, response written length at `+32`, required length at `+36`, sequence low/high at `+40`/`+44`, transport status at `+48`, flags at `+52`, reserved zero at `+56`/`+60`; initial request region `[256, 32768)`, response region `[32768, 65536)`, task allocation starting at `65536`). Guest allocation/growth occurs between `exchange` calls, never inside `exchange`.\n3. **Transport statuses, execution statuses, and `fetch-response` ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3.2):** Emitted service thunks handle transport return statuses `0 COMPLETE`, `1 NEED_SPACE`, `2 BAD_MESSAGE`, `3 ABI_MISMATCH`, and `4 RESOURCE_LIMIT`. On `NEED_SPACE`, the guest thunk resizes the response buffer to the required byte length at `+36` and issues a `fetch-response` for the same sequence number without re-executing or re-sending the semantic transition. Decoded responses carry the private scheduling status (`TRANSFER`, `HOST_SERVICE`, `YIELDED`, `BLOCKED`, `PARKED`, `HALTED`, `ERROR`).\n4. **TLV framing, expanded-canonical-bytes ordering, and Float64/NaN encoding ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3.3):** All request/response payloads and constant/value definitions emitted or decoded by the runtime module obey the single outer framing rule (`u8 tag | u32 payload-byte-length (LE) | payload` for tags `0`–`13`, with no inner counts), sort map keys and set elements by recursively expanded canonical value bytes before tag-`13` handle compression, and encode every Float64 NaN as `0x7ff8000000000000` (`00 00 00 00 00 00 f8 7f` LE).\n5. **Message envelope, register snapshot fields, and compiled vs. service division ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3.4, [`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.1):** Each service request carries the materialized register execution snapshot (`pc`/`registers`/`frames`/`K`/`store-of`/`value`/`halted`/`blocked` plus layout generation) before any service that can collect or park. External continuation export resolves tag-`13` arena references to the payload values in §3.5 or refuses. Pure body control, register moves, and guest closure calls stay compiled in the emitted wasm module; the host service is never allowed to “run the remaining program” to manufacture the expected trace.\n\nThe host-side implementation of `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`, the **single-engine-port rule**, and the\n“one transition implementation, two dispatchers” rule are specified in\n[`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.4.1 and §E.4.2.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Per-image-row compilation, relocation, and artifact-cache law\n\nCompile each independently admitted image into a wasm Module whose body entry functions import\nthe same `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` ABI memory and services. Instantiate it for the receiving task\nand install its functions into the task's imported function table using the runtime embedding\nAPI. This table maps implementation body ids to uniform functions; it is not the guest offset\ntable.\n\nAttach is a staged, append-only transaction:\n\n1. Apply the same contract/validator gates as `attach-image`. Empty images are no-ops; duplicate component R is a no-op. Production admission retains the existing A-to-R derivation check before executable attachment.\n2. Compute offset = held instruction count. Relocate every operand whose opcode-table kind is `:pc`; shift body start/end. Compute the exact combined segment and `register-hash`, and append `[component-R offset length]`.\n3. Compile/instantiate the new component, reserve directory/table capacity, install its body entries, then publish the new code-layout generation atomically. Prefer component-relative constants plus an instance base offset so the same compiled Module can serve different task layouts. Every pc generated from a component-relative coordinate adds the instance base exactly once before entering public state: header/exit pc, payload site/next pc, return-frame site/return pc, and minted closure body-pc. Incoming absolute restore/return pcs are already absolute and must not receive another base. Resolve absolute pcs through the combined body directory and subtract the selected body start for local dispatch.\n4. Preserve pc, registers, frames, K, all pending payloads, stores, and queues. No existing function slot or instruction pc is renumbered; old compiled instances remain reachable. Failure before publish leaves the old task executable. Attach only while wasm is at a materialized boundary.\n\nFor every boundary payload, fetch the instruction as `(nth (:instructions (:segment vm)) (:pc\nvm))` from the current relocated **combined** segment at the materialized absolute site pc. Pass\nthat instruction and current segment/hash/images to `effects/continuation-payload`; never pair\ncomponent-relative site metadata or a component-only segment with absolute payload pcs. The\ncached component image is compilation input, not the current capture-time code space. Even where\nthe live operand is numerically unchanged by relocation, all payload coordinates and validation\ncontext must agree.\n\nAn empty-image creation seeds `:images [[empty-R 0 0]]`, `:hash nil`, `:pc 0`, `:registers []`,\nand `:halted? true`. Attaching a nonempty image of length n at offset 0 keeps the empty row and\nproduces `[[empty-R 0 0] [R 0 n]]`; it does not unhalt the task, resize registers, or initialize\na body. Reset or a valid execution-entry transition performs that separate work. A later capture\nincludes `:images` because the row count is two, even though only one row contains instructions.\nKeep the zero-length row in payload validation and layout operations; it is distinct from the\nrule that attaching an empty image is a no-op.\n\nThere is no machine-code patching and no need to grow an existing wasm Module's function\nsection. The baseline preallocates table capacity (W0 test profile: 65,536 slots) and uses host\ntable installation plus directory append for new functions; exhaustion is a qualified resource\nrefusal. No guest `table.grow`/`table.set` instruction is required. Optional host-API growth\nmust pass the W0 probe on each runtime and must not change the baseline module feature profile.\nDynamic instantiation/attachment occurs outside an active exchange after returning a staged\nhost-work result; the next synchronized state publishes the new layout. The same component bytes\nand import names are used on all runtimes. Old call frames use absolute return-pc and resolve in\nthe grown directory. Captures retain their old segment/hash for validation, but resume sees the\ncurrent code space. Reset preserves offset rows as in the kernel; load-image replaces the code\nspace and resets execution, so old continuation membership checks can fail.\n\nHost-side `spawn-module`, `module/IModuleKernel` closure/resource lifting and lowering, and\nmodule store snapshots are specified in [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.4.4 and\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.3. Cache compiled code by A, r2\nderivation/checksum, wasm ABI/emitter version and feature profile; R alone is not executable\nadmission authority.\n\nArtifact policy is also shared with LLVM: `docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:997–1011,\n1113–1122, 1261–1263`. Wasm binaries are disposable **node-local caches**, never dao.jing\ncontent identities, refs, continuation fields or portable module payloads. Include\ntarget/profile, translator and ABI versions in the cache key alongside the admitted A/R\nprovenance above. No cross-node artifact fetching/sharing is introduced. A missing/dropped\nartifact falls back to the same host’s register interpreter; no compiler or LLVM worker is\nrequired to resume. Install derived artifacts only at materialized boundaries, with no pending\nmutation or active guest stack. Body-relative code and task base offsets follow the same\nrow-local cache rule in both backends.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Compiler toolchain, optimization axis (W6), non-goals, and risks\n\n### 6.1 Toolchain and W0–W6 lowering gates\n\nThe full W0–W6 slice schedule, runtime acceptance gates, and exact toolchain pins (`Node 25.6.1`,\n`Clojure CLI 1.12.4.1602 / shadow-cljs 3.3.5`, `WABT 1.0.37`, `Wasmtime =38.0.0`, `Wasmer 6.0.1`,\n`Rust / Cargo 1.90.0`) are specified in [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5.3 and\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.5.\n\nFor the lowering pipeline specifically:\n- **W0**: Pin the JVM Clojure WAT emitter and WABT `1.0.37` assembler/validator with an explicit baseline feature allowlist; reject every opcode/type outside the baseline allowlist even when an engine enables extensions by default. Binaryen is absent from the baseline toolchain.\n- **W1–W5**: Compile each admitted image once and execute those identical wasm bytes under Node, Wasmtime (from W1), and Wasmer (complete W1–W4 suite in W4, release gate in W5), verifying byte-identical artifact emission and `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` import allowlist compliance.\n- **W6 (measured optimization, after correctness)**: Keep Node + Wasmtime + Wasmer full-trace parity mandatory for every optimization. Measure compilation latency, binary size, native stack depth, arena/heap retention, dispatch cost, serialized message bytes/copy/codec time, call and suspension cost, and end-to-end time on each embedder; compare Node against interpreted register and report native results separately without attributing service-language speed to wasm codegen. Only then coalesce blocks, reduce host crossings, add dirty spilling, or use optional Binaryen passes. Re-run all parity and binary validation gates for each transformation. If crossing cost dominates, prototype the self-contained interpreter-loop variant with native-to-wasm migration of value/scheduler services and only stream/FFI ABI crossings. Compare it against compiled bodies with batched definitions/block dispatch on the same workloads; select by measurements while keeping explicit resumable state. Any service migration reuses/compiles the same Rust crate semantics where the toolchain/profile permits it, rather than authoring a third engine; the i32-handle `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` ABI and full kit remain the contract. K3 may measure wasm on this same harness alongside its JVM/Rust/AOT comparisons; these measurements do not commission K4.\n\n### 6.2 Lowering non-goals and risks\n\nNon-goals for the lowering: no new opcode/contract version, alternate A→R allocator, native\nstack continuations, async-stack capture, universal cross-model continuation conversion, new\nportable continuation schema, direct arbitrary host FFI imports, WASI imports in the VM core,\nthreads/shared memory, WasmGC dependency, numeric NaN boxing, cross-node artifact service, or\noptimization before W5 correctness. Guest-agnostic and register-runtime non-goals are listed in\n[`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5.4 and\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.6.\n\nLowering-specific risks (retained from r5 §7; host/runtime risks in\n[`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5.4 and\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.6):\n- **Large bodies and dispatch size.** One instruction per case can create deep block nesting and large binaries. W0 should pin practical limits; W6 can shard dispatch into bounded pages/helpers while keeping entry-pc precision. Such helpers must never hold suspended guest state in their native stack.\n- **Module growth and code retention.** Wasm module code cannot be appended in place. Many image instances may increase memory and startup cost; function-table limits and compile failures require explicit resource refusals. Code unloading while live absolute pcs exist is out of scope.\n- **Canonical admission and provenance.** Bare hand images remain useful under the existing raw kernel API; production module execution must retain A derivation verification. Compiled wasm caches are disposable artifacts. They cannot become another authority for code or continuation identity.\n\n---\n\n## Reference conventions\n\nReference convention: `register.cljc` means `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc`; `engine`,\n`ffi`, `module`, `values`, and `debruijn_register_effects` filenames are under\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/`; `vm` means `src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc`; `apply2` is the alias for\n`dao.stream.apply` at `src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc`. Unqualified register helpers such as\nreturn-transition, write-back, payload-of, park-entry-fns, attach-image and spawn-module are in\nregister.cljc; spawn-module/gc-roots are protocol implementations, not invented defns.\n`module/IModuleKernel` and its gc-roots method are declared in module.cljc. IVM step/run are\nmethods of `yin.vm/IVM`, not a namespace named IVM. `effects/*` names denote\ndebruijn_register_effects.cljc. `register-image-defect`, `body-liveness`, `live-slot-index`,\n`register-hash` and `opcode-table` belong to `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc`\n(alias rcode); the `register-image` derivation belongs to\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/derive.cljc`. `halted-with-empty-queue?`, `scheduler-round`,\n`check-wait-set`, `handle-close` and `collect` are engine.cljc functions. The quoted\nrestore/call helper names are existing source semantics, whereas exchange/fetch-response, ABI\nstatuses and all native implementation entry names are proposed protocol vocabulary.\n\nThe `.cljc` line references in this specification, [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md)\n(§E.1–§E.5), and [`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) (§10) are the r5-verified\nreferences, checked against the current tree for this distillation. Rust-design line references\nretain r5's coordinates; the related-spec additions to that document do not shift its existing\nlines. New ABI names and native entry names describe planned interfaces, not existing repository\nfunctions.\n\n## Open items\n\nThe frozen r5 text is retained where it leaves this codegen/host-boundary detail in tension;\nthis decomposition does not resolve it (see [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5.5 and\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.6 for the runtime\ntransport-test notation item):\n\n- §4 / [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3.4 (r5 §4.1.1) says guest closure calls stay\n  compiled, while the single-port rule in [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.4.2 (r5\n  §4.1.1) requires service sites to reuse the owning host's call transition. The division\n  between generated dispatch and shared transition work needs to be made concrete without\n  creating a second semantic implementation.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
