{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1137,1296p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md; sed -n '1,155p' docs/design/yang.wasm.md\"","aggregated_output":"#### E.5.2 Cross-embedder encoding, hash, and traversal determinism gates\n\nAcross every embedder (Node/CLJS, Wasmtime, Wasmer) and the shared Rust\ncrates, the conformance vectors enforce the three encoding domains (§E.2.2),\nthe private `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` ABI (§E.3), and the shared deterministic\ntraversal prerequisite (§E.4.3):\n- **Three-domain hash and byte parity (§E.2.2):** Equal test secrets,\n  identities, and inputs yield **bit-identical** R, owner, seal,\n  primitive-profile, and manifest addresses across CLJS and Rust. Reuse\n  `test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.json` and its README/errata plus\n  `test/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc`,\n  `test/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc`,\n  `test/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc`, and\n  `test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code_test.cljc` without regenerating existing\n  goldens; add cross-embedder vectors for `descriptor-hash`/R (including empty\n  and concatenated images), exact encoded bytes, owner/seal/profile inputs,\n  and manifest addresses. W1 verifies load/primitive profiles, W2 recomputes\n  continuation hashes, W3 checks every minted reference seal, and W4 checks\n  compose/attach/layout/module addresses and received slices.\n- **`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` TLV and ordering conformance (§E.3):** Map keys\n  and set elements sort by fully expanded canonical value bytes before handle\n  compression, never by arena-reference bytes; float64 NaN is\n  `0x7ff8000000000000`, with integral floats and `-0.0` preserved by their\n  carriers; each node has exactly one outer\n  `u8 tag | u32 payload-byte-length (LE) | payload` frame, with no inner\n  counts. Duplicate/colliding entries and malformed nodes refuse. Task-local\n  references and trusted descriptors do not confer portable value authority.\n  Native handles resolve through the safe shared `Value` facade, not a\n  separate codec/engine port.\n- **Deterministic service traversal gates (§E.4.3):** Identical minted\n  `:yin.k/c-N` cell ids, stream/cursor ids and seals, `:yin.k/entries`,\n  `:cells`, `:available` vectors, counters, resource maps, and\n  resource-update diagnostic order across equal maps built with different\n  insertion histories, small and large maps spanning CLJS map representations,\n  nested maps/sets, repeated stream/cursor refs, multi-store modules, and\n  multiple invalid update keys.\n\n#### E.5.3 W0 baseline toolchain pins, feature probes, and transport/snapshot gates\n\nDeliver/extend K0's single exported fixture kit and its CLJS,\nRust-interpreter, and wasm adapters, the core-profile feature probe, pinned\nassembler/validator, ABI codec/schema, and operation-by-operation conformance\nfixtures. The following are **exact baseline pins** for W0, not “latest”\nconstraints or a claim of an already working build:\n\n| Tool | Pin / scope | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Node | `25.6.1` | Existing `mise.toml` node pin; keep the repository selection. |\n| Clojure CLI / shadow-cljs | `1.12.4.1602` / `3.3.5` | Existing `mise.toml` and `deps.edn` selections. JVM emitter uses the repository Java 21 lane; record resolved vendor/build in the W0 manifest. |\n| WABT | `1.0.37` | [Release](https://github.com/WebAssembly/wabt/releases/tag/1.0.37); assemble and validate with an explicit baseline feature allowlist. |\n| Wasmtime native crate/runtime | `=38.0.0` | [Versioned embedding API](https://docs.rs/wasmtime/38.0.0/wasmtime/); required native runtime from W1. |\n| Wasmer native runtime | `6.0.1` | [Release](https://github.com/wasmerio/wasmer/releases/tag/v6.0.1); second wrapper and completion gate by W5. |\n| Rust / Cargo | `1.90.0` toolchain | [Release](https://blog.rust-lang.org/2025/09/18/Rust-1.90.0/); host service/wrapper builds, not guest wasm generation. |\n\nW0 must build these pins together, freeze transitive native dependencies in a\nlockfile, record checksums and target triples, and run the ABI smoke tests.\nThey are reproducibility starting points, not assertions that all engines'\nlater features work. If a compatibility or security issue requires a pin\nchange, document the exact replacement and rerun all baseline probes; no\nsilent floating version. Binaryen is absent from the baseline toolchain, and\nany later introduction needs its own pin/profile gate. Browser engine/version\nis recorded when its adapter is qualified; browser API convenience never\nexpands the baseline.\n\n**Feature probes:** Probe imported memory, byte-order/bounds behavior,\nsingle-result `i32` imports, scalar `i64` memory fields, mutable globals,\nactive segments, table installation/`call_indirect`, memory growth/view\nrefresh, and multiple image instances sharing one task memory. Explicitly\nreject every opcode/type outside the baseline allowlist, even when an engine\nenables extensions by default. Test same-binary import/export signatures on\nNode, Wasmtime and Wasmer with no WASI imports. Native custom imports require\nthe embedding harness; invoking a bare runtime CLI with no imports is not this\ntest. Freeze deterministic fixture manifests. No new guest semantics. Gate\nevery nonempty image before codegen. Separate native payload transport from\nlinker export capability.\n\n**W0 baseline, snapshot, codec, and transport acceptance:**\n- **Baseline and snapshot isolation:** All semantic goldens retain their\n  literal vector/address; all derived images retain R and exact live operands.\n  Existing register tests pass unchanged on the reference lane. Invalid\n  contract, Rule R, unknown opcode, cross-body target, bad live set and\n  empty-image cases have identical admission outcomes. Validate a minimal wasm\n  instance and explicit resource-limit diagnostics. Establish matching-layout\n  payload transport and qualified refusal for incompatible layouts. Freeze the\n  map/memory authority protocol (§E.1) and its byte-message equivalents,\n  including external `assoc`/snapshot replacement, old-state replay (resuming\n  one snapshot never mutates another snapshot's frames, values, or state\n  fields) and preservation of unknown engine extension keys; do not defer that\n  contract to performance tuning.\n- **Additional W0 codec acceptance:** Run all three codec domains against\n  frozen bytes and hashes before executing native register images. Include\n  map/set canonical collisions, metadata, numeric carriers, surrogate\n  rejection in repository codecs versus private ABI preservation, deep\n  compound referenced keys with permuted arena ids, canonical NaN and\n  float/integer distinction, and exact outer-only framing (odd text lengths,\n  odd map node count, truncated/extra children). Keep private message-codec\n  and repository hash failures separately diagnosable.\n- **W0 transport acceptance:** Force small/zero response capacity:\n  `COMPLETE→NEED_SPACE→fetch-response`, repeated fetch,\n  acknowledgement/release, wrong sequence/task, repeated sequence with changed\n  content, `BAD_MESSAGE`, `ABI_MISMATCH` and `RESOURCE_LIMIT`. Validate\n  bounds/overlap/overflow and confirm malformed/unsupported requests do not\n  execute a semantic transition. A counter-only test service proves that\n  retained responses do not re-execute the action. These tests run on Node and\n  native harnesses during W0, not first in W5.\n\n**W/K dependency map:** W0 consumes/extends K0; W1 shares K1 pure\nkernel/value/image work; W2 shares K2 payload/restore work plus the required\n`yin-engine` park subset; W3–W4 require the Level-2 library services\ndescribed in Rust §6.3, not a second engine. W5 is wasm release\nqualification, not certification of the full K5 standalone node. W6 can\ncontribute wasm measurements to K3's same corpus/harness; K4 still requires\nthe owner's review of K3\n(`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1167–1182, 1215–1228`). No dependency\nauthorizes unrelated implementation. Register-specific W0–W6 implementation\nslices, per-slice differential acceptance gates, and retained Rust K1–K5 /\nLLVM gates live in\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) `§10.5`.\n\n#### E.5.4 Guest-agnostic non-goals and risks\n\nNon-goals: no second Rust kernel/engine/value/codec port; no K4 LLVM\nimplementation, full K5 standalone node, new Rust network transport,\ncross-node artifact service or Rust `Rc`-layout exposure; no universal\ncross-model continuation conversion, new portable continuation schema, direct\narbitrary host FFI imports, WASI imports in the VM core, threads/shared\nmemory, WasmGC dependency, numeric NaN boxing, a mandatory standalone\nhost-free runtime for W5, or immediate JVM/Dart application bindings; no\npromise to execute malicious wasm from the network; no optimization before W5\ncorrectness.\n\nGuest-agnostic host risks (register-specific risks live in\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.6):\n- **Host boundary overhead.** A `ValueRef` implementation deliberately leaves semantic services in conforming embedders, with mandatory serialized messages even for CLJS. Measure in W6 whether compiled dispatch helps before moving collection/value/store services into wasm; the native host implementation needed for portability is already required by W1–W4.\n- **Persistent public state over mutable memory.** Snapshot aliasing and old-state retention are correctness concerns, not just GC details. Decide in W0 whether each public state owns a copy-on-write arena root or a copied active shadow; require old-state replay tests before optimizing.\n- **Host exceptions and partial effects.** Catch host failures at the adapter boundary for shaped diagnostics, then reproduce the existing public exception behavior. Do not roll back an already appended stream event or retry a completed effect. The mailbox needs a single completion/commit rule.\n- **Predicate drift.** `machine-data?`, value wrappers, integer carriers and effect authorization have evolved beyond old doc prose. Reuse the source helpers, and make ABI tests fail when their contracts change. External plain-data serialization and internal trusted-wrapper values are separate gates.\n- **Single-port dependency.** Native coverage depends on the shared Rust crates, including the substantial Level-2 engine subset needed before W3/W4. A faster parallel port is not a workaround. Keep one transition implementation per host language, one exported K0/W0 kit and explicit shared-reference versioning; duplicate dispatchers must not duplicate semantic code. The `Value` facade and snapshot barriers must preserve check-then-commit and `Rc` ownership while keeping handles task-local.\n- **Host coverage.** Node/CLJS and native Wasmtime/Wasmer are shipping targets under one pinned baseline ABI/profile. Browser and later JVM/Dart application integrations may have different resource limits and must rerun the same parity suite. Native semantic-service drift and ABI codec fidelity are the principal portability risks.\n\n#### E.5.5 Reference conventions and open items\n\nReference convention: `register.cljc` means\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc`; `engine`, `ffi`, `module`,\n`values`, and `debruijn_register_effects` filenames are under\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/`; `vm` means `src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc`; `apply2` is the alias\nfor `dao.stream.apply` at `src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc`. `module/IModuleKernel`\nand its `gc-roots` method are declared in `module.cljc`. `IVM` `step`/`run`\nare methods of `yin.vm/IVM`. `halted-with-empty-queue?`, `scheduler-round`,\n`check-wait-set`, `handle-close` and `collect` are `engine.cljc` functions.\nThe quoted restore/call helper names are existing source semantics, whereas\n`exchange`/`fetch-response`, ABI statuses and all native implementation entry\nnames are proposed protocol vocabulary. Rust-design line references retain\nr5's coordinates (`1–1334`).\n\n**Open items** (retained from r5 without resolution across this specification,\n[`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md), and\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) `§10.6`):\n- §E.3.4 (r5 §4.1.1) says guest closure calls stay compiled, while the single-port rule in §E.4.2 (r5 §4.1.1) requires service sites to reuse the owning host's call transition. The division between generated dispatch and shared transition work needs to be made concrete without creating a second semantic implementation (see also [`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md) `## Open items`).\n- W0 retains the transport-test notation `COMPLETE→NEED_SPACE→fetch-response` (§E.5.3). §E.3.2 (r5 §4.1.1) defines `COMPLETE` and `NEED_SPACE` as alternative `exchange` results; the notation's test sequencing needs clarification, not an ABI change.\n# yang.wasm: lowering register images to WebAssembly modules\n\nStatus: design, owner-authorized planning; not implemented.\n\nConsensus provenance (2026-10-18): gpt-6-astra, author; gemini-4-argon,\nreviewer; five review rounds, APPROVE on r5. Author thread:\n`01a14f30-ca56-7530-bb93-3a870eca7a7b`; reviewer thread:\n`f365ef1d-caa2-4379-8e96-51e64ca36af8`. This specification distills the\ncompiler and target-ABI half of the frozen r5 consensus: semantic\nfidelity, portability, determinism and Rust-kernel reconciliation. It\ndoes not report an implementation or runtime acceptance result.\n\n2026-10-19 decomposition, owner-ruling, and fold notes:\n1. **Decomposition and owner-directed fold (2026-10-19)**: Register-image-to-WebAssembly lowering and all 23\n   opcode lowerings live in this specification (`yang.wasm.md`). By explicit\n   owner ruling, `docs/design/yin.vm.register-wasm.md` and\n   `docs/design/yin.vm.conformance-kit.md` are retired and folded into the\n   existing architecture documents: the guest-agnostic execution host, the\n   canonical `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` service protocol, and the conformance\n   **method** live in [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) (`§E.1–§E.5`);\n   the register-machine wasm execution instance, register fixture schema, and\n   register W0–W6 / Rust K1–K5 lane gates live in\n   [`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) (`§10`).\n2. **Owner ruling (2026-10-19, ABI naming)**: Because the host-independent execution\n   contract belongs to `yin.vm.engine`, the service import ABI is renamed\n   `yin.register.host.v1` → `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`, and its canonical\n   protocol tables live in [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md)\n   `§E.3`. Section 4 below specifies the compiler's target-interface\n   binding to `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` and references those tables. Normative\n   content is otherwise unchanged.\n\n## Authorities\n\n- [datom.world.md](./datom.world.md): foundational axioms and invariants.\n- [yang.antlr.md](./yang.antlr.md): frontend compilation into the Universal AST.\n- [yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md): Universal AST content and projection discipline.\n- [yin.vm.semantic.md](./yin.vm.semantic.md): A identity, semantic execution and A→R derivation.\n- [yin.vm.debruijn.register.md](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md): register contract, live sets, sparse continuations, and (`§10`) the register-machine WebAssembly execution instance (the eight materialized register fields, sparse continuations on wasm, `register-restore`, register fixture schema, and register-specific W0–W6 / Rust K1–K5 gates).\n- [yin.vm.linker.md](./yin.vm.linker.md): admission, offset tables and module transport.\n- [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md): scheduler, effects, custody, and (`§E.1–§E.5`) the guest-agnostic execution host, `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` service protocol, and K0/W0 conformance method.\n- [yin.vm.rust-kernel.md](./yin.vm.rust-kernel.md): shared Rust crates, Value ownership, transition reuse and LLVM relationship.\n\nSource precedence and the explicit Rust-design reconciliations below are\nretained from r5.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Pipeline position, decision and source precedence\n\nPipeline position:\n\n    yang source --(yang.antlr)--> Universal AST --(project)--> semantic vector A\n        --(yin.vm.semantic/derive register-image)--> register image R\n        --(yang.wasm, this spec)--> WebAssembly module (disposable local artifact)\n\n**A is the only code identity.** R is the register image checksum. The\nemitted WebAssembly module is a derived, disposable local execution\nartifact with no identity in `dao.jing`, no content address, and no\npresence in continuation payloads or module manifests.\n\nImplement a **compiled register backend with resumable body functions and an explicit\ntrampoline**. Each declared body becomes a wasm function with one local per physical register.\nIntra-body control initially uses generated `br_table` dispatch; guest calls and returns update\nexplicit machine state and return to the trampoline. No guest continuation ever depends on a\nwasm activation. Target host services through the versioned plain-data linear-memory import ABI\n`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` (§4 below; canonical protocol tables in\n[`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3, r5 §4.1.1). The existing CLJS engine is one\nconforming embedder; a native service implementation shared by Wasmtime and Wasmer adapters is\nanother ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.4,\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10). Neither JS nor a browser is required to\nexecute the same emitted binary.\n\nThe toolchain is a JVM Clojure emitter producing deterministic WAT, assembled with WABT and\ninstantiated unchanged by Node/browser, Wasmtime or Wasmer. This is emission from register\nimages, not compilation of ClojureScript to wasm. W0 names exact toolchain pins\n([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5.3);\nthese are implementation acceptance targets, not tools claimed to be installed or tested here. A\nRust native embedder implements the ABI and semantic services without invoking Node, embedding a\nJS engine, or delegating transitions to a CLJS subprocess. Wasmtime is required beside Node from\nW1; Wasmer joins the release gate by W5. Browser embedding uses the same ABI. JVM and Dart\nremain semantic reference lanes; binding wasm into their application APIs is separate from the\nrequired standalone native embedders.\n\nThe mandatory feature profile is **core wasm 1.0 with mutable globals and the original function\ntable / `call_indirect` facility**: i32/i64/f32/f64, structured control, one non-shared wasm32\nmemory and one function table per module, single-result functions, active data/element segments.\nNo externref or first-class reference values, typed function references, `call_ref`,\n`return_call`, WasmGC, bulk-memory/table instructions, multi-value, multi-memory, memory64,\nSIMD, exceptions, threads, component model or JS string builtins are assumed. The baseline table\nelement kind used by `call_indirect` is not permission to emit the later reference-types\ninstruction set. Copy/clear uses scalar loops; no implicit `memory.copy`, `table.set` or\n`table.grow` instruction may slip in through optimization. Mutable globals need not cross the\nimport boundary; instance base coordinates can use immutable scalar imports.\n\nEvery extension requires a separate named profile, W0 validation/probe on all target engines and\nmatching cache key; the baseline artifact must continue to work without it. WABT/Binaryen\ndefaults are not the feature contract. Uneven native-runtime support for post-MVP features is\nthe reason for this deliberate cut, not an unresolved portability risk. WasmGC remains a\nnon-goal despite its inclusion in the September 2025 Wasm 3.0 standard; standardization does not\nrequire this backend to adopt it. [Wasm 3.0\nannouncement](https://webassembly.org/news/2025-09-17-wasm-3.0/).\n\nWASI stays outside the VM core and its imports. A WASI-based embedder may implement stream\ntransports/FFI below the same ABI; the generated program imports neither WASI nor\nruntime-specific system calls. Binaryen is optional after parity; no wasm-ld pipeline is\nrequired for generated guest code. Native embedder builds have their own pinned native\ntoolchain.\n\nRead authorities (source symbols are semantic specifications for every embedder; calls to\nexisting CLJC helpers below mean direct reuse in CLJS and equivalently tested native\ntransitions, not a requirement for native hosts to load Clojure):\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc`, `debruijn_register_code.cljc`,\n`debruijn_register_effects.cljc`, `semantic/derive.cljc`, `semantic.cljc`, `engine.cljc`,\n`values.cljc`, `yin/vm.cljc`, `linker.cljc`; the register, semantic, linker and engine design\ndocuments; and the semantic and register test corpora. In particular:\n\n- The register contract is version 4 / `\"r2\"`, with exactly 23 opcodes. Preserve the image, physical L/T allocation, instruction pcs, BLAKE3 checksum, and six in-band live operands. Do not allocate another guest register namespace.\n- The October amendments to the register and semantic designs make **A the code identity**. R is the register image checksum. Existing `:hash`, `:image`, and offset rows still use R for native compatibility. Do not publish wasm binaries as a new code identity, fetch code by a wasm checksum, or bypass A-to-R admission. Retain access to the Universal AST through existing provenance/addressing.\n- Older paragraphs in register §5.2 still put code-space fields on return frames and restore code from payloads. Linker §7.3 r6/r7 and the implementation supersede them: return frames contain no code space; restore never rewinds it. Engine documentation (`yin.vm.engine.md` §1.1, §2.1, §3, corrected 2026-10-19) also contained historical `:store-updates` wording; current ready entries update private `:resources` through `:resource-updates`.\n- Current `vm/machine-data?`, used by register restore and payload validation, recursively accepts kernel-minted Closure/Continuation wrappers by their payloads, as well as exact-integer carriers. It is not identical to `plain-data?`. Arbitrary host functions/errors remain invalid. Preserve the predicate, not an approximate JSON test.\n- The current module-slice encoder in `engine/encoder` explicitly refuses reified and parked continuations with `:yin.k/status :yin.k/non-portable`, kind `:non-canonicalizable`. Thus universal linker transport of every register continuation is not already implemented. This lane must support the existing native payload where admissible, and preserve qualified refusals elsewhere; it must not silently promise a new universal migration mechanism.\n\n### 1.1 Rust-kernel authority, scope and explicit reconciliations\n\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md`](./yin.vm.rust-kernel.md) revision 2 is an authority for\nthe native implementation: status and subordination at lines 3–35, crates at 1134–1158,\ndecisions at 1234–1265. It remains **design-only, not authorized for implementation**. This\nspecification, [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) (§E.1–§E.5), and\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) (§10) record the owner-authorized wasm plan;\nthey neither commission K4 LLVM nor the unrelated standalone Rust node/transports. Where native\nownership, Value, transition reuse, fault isolation and crate boundaries govern, this plan is\nsubordinate to that design. Crate/type names below are proposed by that document, not claims of\nimplemented Rust symbols.\n\nThis plan explicitly **supersedes only the DEFERRED item “WASM as an artifact target”**\n(`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1294–1300`). Its other deferred items and K3-owner-review\nprerequisite for K4 remain. The following narrower reconciliations prevent silent\ncontradictions:\n\n- Current A identity and A→R admission above govern over the older Rust topology and R-as-identity wording (§3, lines 282–318; §7.5, lines 997–1011). R remains the exact executable checksum/offset-row token. Neither backend creates an identity or bypasses canonical admission.\n- Rust K-D2b uses u32 indices and qualified host limits (lines 434–448, 1245–1246); wasm keeps 64-bit **wire pc fields**, not a promise of unrestricted native execution. The native facade checks every pc/operand/attachment against the Rust domain and declared limits before conversion or mutation and returns the same qualified `:host-limit`. W0 records the common accepted profile; tests outside it require declared refusals, never truncation or a second widened Rust Machine. Wide register tests above 65,535 still fit u32.\n- Rust phases exclude existing CLJC edits (lines 93–95, 1164–1165). The deterministic service traversal policy in [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.4.3 (r5 §4.1.2) is a separately identified **wasm-driven shared-reference stabilization prerequisite**, outside that K-phase restriction; it remains an explicit scoped amendment for implementation review, not permission for a second native ordering policy. Export a versioned extension of the common kit after that shared change, preserving original goldens/history. This specification changes no source.\n- Rust public `step` always interprets (lines 677–678). Keep that rule for the Rust interpreter lane. The wasm backend's explicit one-instruction test/inspection mode is a different dispatcher used to verify its generated execution; it does not silently replace Rust's public `step`. Optimized wasm run chunks obey the same boundary equivalence law.\n- The native wasm embedder needs Level-2 engine services in W2–W4 even though the Rust standalone-node roadmap leaves K5 last (§6.3, lines 748–781; §10, lines 1170–1182). Develop/reuse those **same crates** to the slice's required coverage ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.4.2); a Level-1 offload service cannot satisfy native standalone wasm parity. This schedules the necessary library subset for wasm, not the full K5 node or a new Rust network client.\n\nOwner question 1 (lines 1282–1284) and C1/A3 (lines 243–258, 1275–1277) align with the owner's\ninstruction to lower held register images: wasm is a kernel execution strategy over an admitted\nimage, not a replacement source emitter. Do not reopen that choice or infer authorization for\nLLVM. The node-local cache choice aligns with question 2; compile farms remain out of scope.\nWorker envelope choice and K3 commissioning threshold remain with the LLVM work; W0's declared\nscalar profile fixes only this lane's supported domain, not all future Rust profiles (questions\nat 1285–1292).\n\n---\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '156,341p' docs/design/yang.wasm.md; tail -8 docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md; git log -4 --oneline -- docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md; rg -n 'store-updates|correction|Correction' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md; rg -n 'yin\\\\.register\\\\.host\\\\.v1|yin\\\\.vm\\\\.(register-wasm|conformance-kit)\\\\.md' docs/design/*.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '156,341p' docs/design/yang.wasm.md; tail -8 docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md; git log -4 --oneline -- docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md; rg -n 'store-updates|correction|Correction' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md; rg -n 'yin\\\\.register\\\\.host\\\\.v1|yin\\\\.vm\\\\.(register-wasm|conformance-kit)\\\\.md' docs/design/*.md\"","aggregated_output":"## 2. Emitted module contract and linear-memory layout\n\n### 2.1 Values in emitted code: i32 ValueRef locals\n\nChoose **i32 ValueRef locals**, not NaN boxing and not externref registers. Reserve 0 = nil, 1 =\nfalse, 2 = true; all other values use checked arena indices. Every integer, float, string,\nsymbol, keyword, collection, primitive, closure, continuation, and sealed stream/cursor\nreference is an arena entry. Numeric computation initially remains in embedder primitive\nservices: the CLJS adapter uses existing implementations, and the native service implements the\nsame declared primitive profiles, integer carriers, float behavior, equality and metadata\nsemantics. W0 codec/profile fixtures prohibit substituting Rust or JS default numeric/coercion\nrules. Zero, empty strings, and empty collections remain truthy: only references 0 and 1 are\nfalse.\n\nEach embedder owns an explicit per-task arena, not a process global. Linear memory contains a\nparallel descriptor table: kind, generation, opaque host-slot and optional machine-record\noffset. The slot is a task-local numeric identity, never a JS object address or native pointer.\nAll service exchanges serialize plain-data messages in linear memory under\n`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` (§4 below; [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3, r5 §4.1.1),\nincluding in Node; there is no privileged direct-object fast path. Known immutable arena values\nmay be referenced by checked task-local ids in those messages, with definitions transmitted on\nfirst use.\n\nUsing i32 handles avoids passing i64 values as JS numbers or requiring externref persistence. It\ncosts host crossings and arena indirection. This is acceptable for the first semantic\nimplementation: register instructions themselves contain no numeric arithmetic opcode. A later\nnative scalar fast path needs explicit numeric laws and benchmarks, not a change to the public\nimage contract.\n\nHost-side value reconstruction, the native `handle↔Value` facade over `yin-value`'s 16-byte\n`#[repr(C, u8)]` `Value` enum (`K-D2`), and the three mandatory encoding/hashing domains\n(`yin-scalar`, `yin-image`, `yin-wire`) are specified in\n[`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.2.1 and §E.2.2.\n\n### 2.2 Private linear-memory layout\n\nUse imported, non-shared wasm32 memory, little-endian and bounds checked. One task owns one\nmemory arena; all its compiled image instances import that arena. Separate tasks receive\nseparate arenas even when compiled Module objects are cached. A supplied coremem can implement\nthe same interface only with task isolation and allocation ownership; shared mutable guest state\nis not introduced.\n\n| Region / record | Contents and rules |\n|---|---|\n| Task header | ABI version, run status, absolute pc as unsigned 64-bit field, body id, active register count/pointer, lexical-chain pointer, return-stack pointer, store-of ValueRef, value ValueRef, host-state token, code-layout generation, scratch/request pointers. Pointer fields are u32. |\n| Body directory | Absolute start/end pcs, register/local counts, image row, compiled function slot, body-relative entry mapping. Append-only while attached. |\n| Active register shadow | Dense array of u32 ValueRefs of exactly the current body's `:registers` size. L slots are initialized from positional arguments; T slots start nil. Wasm locals cache this array while a body executes. |\n| Lexical frames | Immutable vector records: count followed by ValueRefs. Chain is an immutable outermost-first vector of frame pointers. Depth d indexes `count - 1 - d`, then position. |\n| Return stack | Immutable/persistent vector of return-record pointers, innermost last. Records contain site-pc, return-pc, frames pointer, sparse pairs pointer, live vector pointer, dest, optional store-of. No segment/hash/layout snapshot. |\n| Sparse snapshots | Count followed by sorted `(u32 register-index, u32 ValueRef)` pairs. Records do not alias mutable active-register storage. Only the verified live set is captured. |\n| Continuation record | Immutable snapshot metadata: format/hash/image ValueRefs, capture-time segment and optional images ValueRefs, site-pc, pc, frames, sparse regs/live, return stack, destination presence/index, resume mode, optional store-of. Engine/FFI transport fields remain losslessly represented in the host payload. |\n| Value descriptors | Kind/generation/host slot plus optional closure or continuation record pointer. Host slot contains the actual immutable value or trusted wrapper. |\n| Engine state roots | ValueRefs/tokens for store, module-stores, free-env, primitives, modules, wait-set, ready-queue, parked, resources, heap/gc, owner, origin/ancestry, id-counter, `:yin.k/gate`, `:yin.k/issued`, and `:yin.k/closes`. Preserve all other extension keys as well. The actual issue-counter key is `:yin.k/issued`, distinct from id-counter; do not invent `:yin.k/next-issue`. These are state/arena retention roots, not an expansion of the engine’s logical heap-GC root set. The full existing state map is authoritative for engine extension keys. |\n| Exit mailbox | Pending instruction site/op and argument refs, pending result/error ref, completion kind. Private implementation state, never a portable continuation. |\n\nPcs use 64-bit fields so wasm32 pointers do not silently narrow the existing layout domain (up\nto `2^52-1` in `compose`). Functions use small body-relative i32 dispatch indices after checked\nresolution. Reject resource/address exhaustion with a qualified backend diagnostic before\nallocation/attach; never wrap a pc, handle, or byte offset. The counter remains the existing\nhost integer value and advances only through engine operations.\n\nThe program store is logically an immutable map, usually name-keyed but **not restricted to\nnames**: `:store-get`/`:store-put` accept `:data` keys, including keywords and numbers in the\ncorpus. Its linear-memory representation is a root ValueRef to the embedder’s persistent map,\nnot a second wasm hash map; the map and updates are addressable through ABI value\ndefinitions/state messages on every runtime. All writes go through `engine/put-active` →\n`engine/store-put`. Non-nil store-of selects only that module's store, with no ambient fallback.\nPrivate resources and heap are distinct from program store.\n\nThe guest-agnostic machine-state-as-data synchronization barrier is specified in\n[`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.1 and §E.2.3; the register machine's eight-field\ninstance and dense-register shadow spilling rules are specified in\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.1.1 and §10.1.2.\n\n### 2.3 Module imports, exports and deterministic emission\n\nThe instance exports conceptual `run(state, budget)`, `step(state)`, `read-state(state)` and\nvalidated state-install operations. The shared runtime module imports `memory` (one wasm32,\nnon-shared memory), `functions` (one original function table), and\n**`exchange(control_ptr: i32) -> i32`** from module name `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` (renamed from\n`yin.register.host.v1` by owner ruling on 2026-10-19; see §4 and\n[`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3). Generated component modules import the same\nmemory/table and the service entry as needed, plus declared immutable i32 base-offset words. No\nimport/export passes a JS value, externref, Rust pointer, string object, multi-value result or\ni64 through a host-call signature. i64 pcs/numbers are bytes in memory; core i64 computation\ninside wasm is allowed.\n\nUse finite status values: TRANSFER (continue trampoline), HOST_SERVICE (complete current\ninstruction), YIELDED (budget), BLOCKED, PARKED, HALTED, ERROR. These statuses are private\nscheduling results; exported VM maps retain exact `:blocked?`, `:halted?`, `:value`, wait-set,\nready-queue, and parked behavior. **Blocking is a normal return, never a trap.** Unexpected wasm\nvalidation/memory traps are backend faults and never substitutes for `:yin/blocked` or an FFI\nerror envelope.\n\nEmission is byte-deterministic: equal admitted register image, emitter/ABI version, and feature\nprofile yield byte-identical WAT and assembled `.wasm` binaries across runs and platforms.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Code generation strategy and all 23 opcode lowerings\n\n### 3.1 Choice among the alternatives\n\n| Strategy | Benefits | Costs / semantic obligations |\n|---|---|---|\n| Fully structured body functions with direct guest `call` | Small fast code for ordinary functions; conventional optimizer input | Captured continuations require every caller activation to be materialized and abandoned; arbitrary resume PCs need secondary entries; suspension cannot retain native stack frames. A shadow stack alone does not unwind native callers. |\n| One interpreter loop over instruction records | Small wasm binary, trivial append and pc resume | Runtime operand decoding and memory-indexed registers on every step; does not exploit known register operands as locals. Uses the same K0/W0 oracle; retained as a W6 optimization axis with services moved into wasm if serialized crossing cost dominates. |\n| **Compiled body dispatch plus trampoline** | Static register locals and operands, arbitrary entry PCs, appendable bodies, constant native stack depth, explicit continuations | More generated code than interpreter; indirect dispatch and spills at transfers; per-body branch tables may be large. |\n\nChoose the third. A body function has conceptual signature `(state-ptr i32, entry-index i32,\nbudget i32) -> status i32`; updated budget/state lives in the header. All bodies share the same\nsignature for a function table. The trampoline resolves absolute pc, calls one body function,\nthen loops on transfer. A body **never invokes a guest callee directly**. Guest call saves a\nreturn frame (unless tail), installs callee state, and returns TRANSFER. Return installs caller\nstate and returns TRANSFER; final return returns HALTED. The trampoline's wasm `call_indirect`\nis an implementation dispatch, not the guest call stack. On HOST_SERVICE the body has returned;\na shared runtime service thunk serializes the mailbox and invokes the `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`\nimport ABI (§4; [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3). It decodes the completed response\nor returns the blocked/error status. No guest activation remains live across an asynchronous\nwait. The same thunk and body binaries run on each embedder.\n\nConsequently flattening is exact and incremental: every pending guest call is already a sparse\nmemory record before its callee runs. Capturing K copies/shares those immutable records;\nabortive application replaces the current chain and returns from the one active body. No stack\nwalk, Asyncify, native-stack copying, wasm exception unwinding, or retained JS Promise\ncontinuation is needed.\n\n`return_call` is **not required or emitted**. Tail guest calls retain K unchanged and replace\nthe active lexical chain/register file. This gives bounded native stack depth on engines without\nthe tail-call extension. A later direct-call optimization may be allowed only for a proven\nnoncapturing, nonsuspending region with a deoptimization protocol; it is not part of this plan.\n\n### 3.1.1 LLVM sibling and shared equivalence law\n\nThe wasm path and Rust K4 LLVM path are sibling execution strategies under\n`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:805–831` (§7.2): from equal reachable boundary states they\nreach equal later states, ordered effects and refusal diagnostics. Both use body functions,\nentry-pc dispatch, live sets as spill maps and data guest calls; their native control\ninstructions differ, their guest semantics do not (§7.3, lines 833–918). W0’s shared kit tests\nall lanes; neither translator forks the validator, liveness rules, payload schema or semantic\nservice implementation. An artifact may be discarded at a materialized boundary and execution\ncontinued by the owning interpreter from the same state. Add wasm→Rust interpreter and wasm→CLJS\ninterpreter fallback fixtures; no artifact field may be needed to restore.\n\n### 3.2 Structuring algorithm\n\n1. Admit the image with the exact contract check and `register-image-defect`. Preserve body ranges and the original source instruction vector as metadata. Reuse `body-liveness`/`live-slot-index`; do not derive live sets from the optimized wasm graph.\n2. Build each body's CFG using the existing successor rules: conditional two-way edge; jump target; no successor for return/halt/resume or tail call; ordinary call and other operations resume at pc+1. Jumps must remain inside their body. Do not assume a hand-authored valid image is the structured syntax emitted by `derive`.\n3. For W1, make every instruction an entry case. Load locals from shadow, enter a wasm `loop` containing nested void `block`s and a `br_table` selecting the pc case. Each case consumes its operands into locals, leaves an empty operand stack, updates the next pc/case, then branches to dispatch or returns one status. All branch targets have the same empty result type. Functions return their status only at the common exit. Invalid entry takes a qualified fault path, not execution of arbitrary memory.\n4. For W6, merge straight-line instruction cases into basic blocks, retaining leaders at targets, body entries, all resume pcs, and every externally observable step/exit site. Preserve an entry path for each supported pc; debug one-step mode can retain instruction cases. General irreducible CFGs keep dispatch. A subsequent measured optimization can compute dominators/SCCs and structure single-entry regions as `block`/`loop`/`if`, retaining a local dispatcher for multi-entry SCCs. There is no need to ship a full relooper to get a correct backend.\n5. Check budget at instruction boundaries for bounded/debug execution. A budget yield materializes the **full** active state and returns YIELDED; it is not a new guest suspension site, has no invented sparse live operand, and is not serializable as an r2 continuation. `step` counts one guest instruction, even when several host service transitions implement it.\n\n### 3.3 All 23 opcode lowerings\n\nHere `r` means ValueRef local, `next` means pc+1, and “host service” means a synchronous\n`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` ABI message exchange ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3)\nimplementing the specified engine transition after returning from the body, not an application\ncallback or new external FFI path. The mailbox records the already-read operands so re-entry\nnever repeats an effect.\n\n| Opcode | Lowering and observable behavior |\n|---|---|\n| `:const rd value` | Load interned literal reference into rd; next. Preserve actual scalar/collection value and metadata. |\n| `:load-bound rd depth position` | Read frame chain at `count-1-depth`, then position. Preserve `:load-bound-depth` and `:load-bound-position` failures. Never substitute reserved L locals for the runtime chain. |\n| `:load-free rd name` | Host `engine/resolve-var` with free-env, active store, primitives, modules. Preserve lookup order, present-nil bindings, Rule R, and module resolution. Write rd; next. |\n| `:closure rd arity body-pc` | Mint owned Closure with type, arity, absolute body-pc, immutable captured frames, and optional store-of. Write rd; next. Captured frames outlive active body. |\n| `:move rd rs` | Read rs before writing rd; next. |\n| `:call rd fn-reg arg-regs tail? live` | Read operator and ordered arguments first. Use `engine/operator-kind`. Closure: nil-fill/drop extras to arity, append bound frame to captured chain, initialize L args and nil T slots; push exact sparse caller frame only for non-tail; set closure store-of and body pc; TRANSFER. Host fn: apply f to args. A typed effect first passes `engine/check-callee-effect!`, then follows the complete call-effect contract below (including blocked results and site builders). An ordinary result writes rd/next or executes return transition for tail. Continuation: `engine/operator-kind` checks ownership first, then `engine/continuation-argument` checks exactly one arg, then check format/image, abort current K and write back captured state; no external data gate on this internal path. |\n| `:branch-false cond target` | Branch only when nil/false; all other values next. Empty operand stack on either edge. |\n| `:jump target` | Set pc/case to the verified same-body target. |\n| `:return rs` | Read value. If K nonempty, pop frame, nil-initialize caller register vector, restore saved pairs and dest, frames and store-of, set value and return-pc; TRANSFER. Else set only value, halted true, store-of nil: keep pc at the executing return instruction and preserve active registers, frames, empty K and all other fields. The return-result restore case first changes pc as specified in [`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.2.2 (r5 §3.3). |\n| `:halt rs` | Set value, halted true, store-of nil. Does not pop K as return would; preserve pc at the halt instruction (no increment), active registers, frames, K, blocked flag and all other fields. |\n| `:store-get rd key` | Read literal key from active store, absent → nil; rd/next. |\n| `:store-put rd key value` | Write the **literal value operand**, not a source register, via put-active; return that value in rd; next. |\n| `:define rd name rs` | Read rs, put-active under literal name, write rd; next. Never resolve the definition operator. |\n| `:gensym rd prefix` | Engine gensym using the same task counter as stream/park ids; rd/next. |\n| `:stream-make rd capacity` | Mint authorized `:stream/make` effect; engine calls supplied make-stream. Preserve default-capacity handling; rd/next. Nonblocking under current contract. |\n| `:stream-put rd sr vr live` | Build site payload; typed put effect from read sr/vr. On success rd/result and next. On full enqueue exact writer wait entry, advance pc, BLOCKED. Other outcomes follow engine. |\n| `:stream-cursor rd sr` | Typed cursor effect, engine resource/cell handling, rd/next; nonblocking. |\n| `:stream-next rd cr live` | Build site payload; typed next effect. Immediate result → rd/next; blocked → reader wait entry, advance pc, BLOCKED; all other outcomes follow engine. |\n| `:stream-close rd sr` | Typed close effect and engine result, rd/next; nonblocking. |\n| `:current-continuation rd live` | Snapshot before writing rd at next pc, mint owned Continuation with `:type :reified-continuation`, write rd; next. Destination is excluded from saved live set. |\n| `:park rd live` | Snapshot and advance pc, call engine park-continuation. Adds id/type to parked record, increments counter, sets value to record and halted true. PARKED status; do not invent a stream wait. |\n| `:resume parked-id rs` | Literal keyword id and value from rs. Engine resume-continuation performs custody gate/missing-id checks and removes parked entry, then register restore. Abort current path; no next-pc fallthrough. |\n| `:ffi-call rd op arg-regs live` | Require call pair first; snapshot and read ordered args; pin outgoing refs; park under correlated call-id and advance pc; append request; enter reader wait or full writer wait. Always yields to protocol; no direct call of bridge handler from wasm. |\n\n   (`:segment`). That is the existing contract, not introduced here; a\n   Rust node shares the `Arc<Image>` in memory and pays the cost only on\n   the wire.\n\nRelated specs: [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) (§E.1–§E.5, guest-agnostic execution host, `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` ABI, and K0/W0 conformance method); [yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md) (register-image-to-wasm lowering); [yin.vm.debruijn.register.md](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) (§10, register wasm execution instance, K0/W0 register fixture schema, and register W0–W6 / Rust K1–K5 lane gates).\n\n2026-10-18: Added the owner-authorized wasm-plan and shared-kit pointers; revision 2 status and all other normative content are unchanged.\n2026-10-19: Owner-directed decomposition and fold of the 2026-10-18 wasm consensus into yang.wasm.md (lowering), yin.vm.engine.md §E.1–§E.5 (guest-agnostic execution host, service protocol yin.vm.engine.host.v1, and conformance method), and yin.vm.debruijn.register.md §10 (register wasm execution instance, register fixture schema, and register W0–W6 / Rust K1–K5 lane gates), retiring yin.vm.register-wasm.md and yin.vm.conformance-kit.md; revision 2 status and line coordinates 1–1334 are unchanged.\n7:2026-10-19 correction note: In the 2026-09-22 body below (§1.1, §2.1, §3),\n8:historical `:store-updates` / `{:rule :store-update-key}` wording has been\n75:(historically written `:store-updates` in the 2026-09-22 draft; corrected\n147:`:store-updates` in the 2026-09-22 draft; corrected 2026-10-19), `:blocked?`\n198:*(2026-10-19 correction: `:resource-updates` replaces the 2026-09-22 draft's\n199:`:store-updates` to match `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc`.)*\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:17:`yin.register.host.v1` by owner ruling on 2026-10-19). Sections 1–8 remain\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:379:   service ABI is renamed `yin.register.host.v1` → `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:381:   `docs/design/yin.vm.register-wasm.md` and\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:382:   `docs/design/yin.vm.conformance-kit.md` are retired and folded into the\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:387:     `yin.vm.conformance-kit.md` `§1–§5`, `§8`).\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:390:     `yin.vm.register-wasm.md` `§1–§5`) together with the register fixture\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:392:     `yin.vm.conformance-kit.md` `§2`, `§6`, `§7`).\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:624:ABI is named `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`, renamed from `yin.register.host.v1` in\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:1048:conformance **method** previously housed in `docs/design/yin.vm.conformance-kit.md`\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:1231:>    `yin.register.host.v1` in r5 §4.1.1 by explicit owner ruling on 2026-10-19).\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:1236:> 3. By explicit owner ruling (2026-10-19), neither `yin.vm.register-wasm.md`\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:1237:>    nor `yin.vm.conformance-kit.md` exists as a separate `yin.vm.*` document:\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:16:   owner ruling, `docs/design/yin.vm.register-wasm.md` and\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:17:   `docs/design/yin.vm.conformance-kit.md` are retired and folded into the\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:26:   `yin.register.host.v1` → `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`, and its canonical\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:236:`yin.register.host.v1` by owner ruling on 2026-10-19; see §4 and\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:407:`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` — renamed from `yin.register.host.v1` in r5 §4.1.1 — and its canonical\ndocs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1338:2026-10-19: Owner-directed decomposition and fold of the 2026-10-18 wasm consensus into yang.wasm.md (lowering), yin.vm.engine.md §E.1–§E.5 (guest-agnostic execution host, service protocol yin.vm.engine.host.v1, and conformance method), and yin.vm.debruijn.register.md §10 (register wasm execution instance, register fixture schema, and register W0–W6 / Rust K1–K5 lane gates), retiring yin.vm.register-wasm.md and yin.vm.conformance-kit.md; revision 2 status and line coordinates 1–1334 are unchanged.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
